00:12:55.359 --> 00:13:00.980
Acoustic guitars, banjos, bass guitars,
electric guitars of all sorts.

00:13:01.047 --> 00:13:04.250
Brand new, as well as
items coming in from the public.

00:13:04.317 --> 00:13:06.023
You know, there's Baby Grand.

00:13:07.250 --> 00:13:09.115
-Oh, you got a huge amp.

00:13:09.182 --> 00:13:11.390
-What a great place you guys got.
-Yeah.

00:13:11.457 --> 00:13:14.708
-Where else should I see in Lewiston?
-Um, let's see.

00:13:14.775 --> 00:13:16.762
There's all sorts of places but

00:13:16.829 --> 00:13:19.507
I mean, just walk down this strip
you'll be surprised.

00:13:19.574 --> 00:13:21.334
-It's a trip.
-Yes, it is.

00:13:21.401 --> 00:13:24.156
It's more diverse here
than anywhere else in Maine.

00:13:24.223 --> 00:13:26.732
And this has changed a lot over the years.

00:13:26.799 --> 00:13:29.299
Used to be, you know,
all apartment buildings here.

00:13:29.366 --> 00:13:31.366
There was a pawn shop every block.

00:13:32.954 --> 00:13:35.384
-There you go.
Nice little find here in Lewiston.

00:13:35.451 --> 00:13:38.887
Lewiston Pawn Shop.
Amps, guitars, tons of tools.

00:13:39.419 --> 00:13:40.415
Wonder Bread.

00:13:41.069 --> 00:13:43.222
Looks like the big job in town.

00:13:44.274 --> 00:13:47.671
The streets over here
smell like freshly baked bread.

00:13:48.684 --> 00:13:50.050
Potentially pushing it out.

00:13:50.117 --> 00:13:52.959
[motorcycle revs and drives past]

00:13:55.085 --> 00:13:56.261
All these vents just

00:13:57.678 --> 00:13:59.252
getting the taste buds going.

00:13:59.838 --> 00:14:02.605
So there's an Italian deli bakery up here

00:14:03.380 --> 00:14:07.218
that's been around forever
and really good, the guy said.

00:14:07.285 --> 00:14:08.697
So let's check that out.

00:14:09.887 --> 00:14:14.159
And then you can see right off
the main street goes apartment buildings.

00:14:14.226 --> 00:14:15.254
What's up, man?

00:14:15.321 --> 00:14:16.386
[man] Yo, man.

00:14:16.453 --> 00:14:17.394
What's going on?

00:14:17.461 --> 00:14:19.461
[man speaking inaudibly]

00:14:21.952 --> 00:14:24.537
-I'm not in the military, bro.
-You look like it.

00:14:24.604 --> 00:14:26.432
-I look like a cop. I know, but I'm not.

00:14:26.499 --> 00:14:28.145
I was in the military.

00:14:29.300 --> 00:14:31.299
-Where'd you come from?
-Angola.

00:14:31.366 --> 00:14:33.213
-Angola? Wow.

00:14:33.280 --> 00:14:34.473
-I was in the Navy.

00:14:34.540 --> 00:14:36.690
-Can you come down the stairs?
-Yeah, I can.

00:14:36.757 --> 00:14:37.593
Come on down.

00:14:37.660 --> 00:14:38.881
-Just a second.
-Alright.

00:14:41.133 --> 00:14:42.445
-You're filming me?!
-Yeah.

00:14:42.512 --> 00:14:45.020
Are you dying here
just smelling this bread all the time?

00:14:45.087 --> 00:14:46.629
-Yeah, yeah.
-Peter.

00:14:46.696 --> 00:14:48.660
It makes me eat bread
all the time, man.

00:14:48.727 --> 00:14:51.553
I'm making a video on all of Maine.
A series about Maine.

00:14:51.620 --> 00:14:54.301
And I just listen to the locals.
Get your take.

00:14:54.368 --> 00:14:56.990
Coming from Angola,
what's your take on Lewiston?

00:14:57.057 --> 00:14:58.223
What are your thoughts?

00:14:58.290 --> 00:15:02.418
Well, Lewiston is a small city,
but a nice city. I love to be here.

00:15:02.485 --> 00:15:04.032
It's good to be here.

00:15:04.099 --> 00:15:06.059
-Angola is beautiful, right?
-Yeah.

00:15:06.126 --> 00:15:08.001
It's beautiful, nice country.

00:15:08.068 --> 00:15:09.706
-Yeah, growing.
-Okay.

00:15:09.786 --> 00:15:11.443
And then why you end up in Maine?

00:15:11.510 --> 00:15:12.981
It's so cold here.

00:15:13.515 --> 00:15:18.138
Well, actually, I came to Maine
because I got some friends here.

00:15:18.205 --> 00:15:20.772
It was easy to me to come here
because they're here.

00:15:20.839 --> 00:15:21.672
-Okay. Cool.

00:15:21.739 --> 00:15:25.323
Different parts of the country,
there's not much people that I know.

00:15:25.390 --> 00:15:28.881
Angola, you speak…
Sorry, bro, it's Portuguese or French?

00:15:28.948 --> 00:15:30.998
-Portuguese.
-You speak… I got that right.

00:15:31.065 --> 00:15:32.993
-Obrigado.
-Obrigado.

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:34.348
[laughs]

00:15:34.415 --> 00:15:37.176
-Nice to meet you.
-One of my best friends is Brazilian.

00:15:37.243 --> 00:15:40.069
Ah, Brazilian. Okay.
He teaches Portuguese to you.

00:15:40.136 --> 00:15:41.258
Yeah. Okay, Mr. Peter.

00:15:41.325 --> 00:15:43.357
-You were in the military.
-Yeah, I was.

00:15:43.424 --> 00:15:44.777
-Salute.
-Salute.

00:15:44.844 --> 00:15:45.994
-See you.
-Take care.

00:15:46.061 --> 00:15:46.894
Yes, sir.

00:15:59.889 --> 00:16:02.480
Look at these cool old buildings here.

00:16:02.547 --> 00:16:04.455
I'm sure this was a corner store.

00:16:04.949 --> 00:16:07.590
The owner knew everyone type of thing.

00:16:08.064 --> 00:16:09.878
And now it looks like… I don't know.

00:16:09.945 --> 00:16:13.144
It's a fusion of older buildings,

00:16:14.106 --> 00:16:17.472
somewhat newer apartment buildings.

00:16:18.909 --> 00:16:20.556
Got a little garden in town.

00:16:24.604 --> 00:16:26.428
Is the Italian bakery this way?

00:16:27.178 --> 00:16:28.060
Italian bakery?

00:16:29.003 --> 00:16:30.349
No? Okay.

00:16:42.500 --> 00:16:44.347
How is this over here?

00:16:44.414 --> 00:16:47.764
-This guy's like custard?
-That's a raspberry almond shortbread.

00:16:47.831 --> 00:16:49.686
Looks good. What about this guy?

00:16:49.753 --> 00:16:52.252
That is a ricotta square.

00:16:52.319 --> 00:16:54.283
-Okay. I'll do the shortbread.
-Yeah.

00:16:54.350 --> 00:16:56.583
-Everyone spoke about you guys.
-Nice.

00:16:56.653 --> 00:16:58.253
Comes to $5.39.

00:16:58.834 --> 00:17:00.353
Let's go $30 on that, huh?

00:17:00.420 --> 00:17:02.799
-That's what you want to see, right?
-Thank you.

00:17:02.866 --> 00:17:04.266
What do people tip normally?

00:17:04.333 --> 00:17:06.846
Normally, it's like two or three bucks.

00:17:06.913 --> 00:17:07.834
-Oh, yeah?

00:17:07.901 --> 00:17:10.173
Two or three for something like this?

00:17:10.240 --> 00:17:11.605
-Yeah. I mean…
-That's good.

00:17:11.672 --> 00:17:13.433
Something's better than nothing.

00:17:13.500 --> 00:17:15.378
-But that's good.
-Right.

00:17:16.098 --> 00:17:17.550
That's awesome. That adds up.

00:17:17.617 --> 00:17:19.000
Take care, man. Thank you.

00:17:25.954 --> 00:17:28.611
This place is a trip. That is for sure.

00:17:29.755 --> 00:17:31.696
Sir, is that a mosque over there?

00:17:34.281 --> 00:17:37.180
Not much English with some of these guys.

00:17:37.247 --> 00:17:41.423
Some speak really well and others,
they seem like they're new here.

00:17:41.490 --> 00:17:42.812
What happened?

00:17:42.879 --> 00:17:44.839
They just kept parking in our yard.

00:17:45.354 --> 00:17:47.295
People like walk in our yard.

00:17:48.314 --> 00:17:50.587
-Oh, people going to the mosque.
-Yeah.

00:17:50.654 --> 00:17:55.241
This lady's got a walkie talkie
and this dude's got a walkie talkie.

00:17:55.308 --> 00:17:58.404
They've just been like
protecting the parking lots.

00:17:58.471 --> 00:18:01.059
-Oh, okay. Okay.
It's a parking lot situation.

00:18:04.627 --> 00:18:05.940
This is fantastic.

00:18:09.773 --> 00:18:12.697
So, I think there's some
parking drama going on.

00:18:13.330 --> 00:18:16.369
He was saying the business,
they were parking there.

00:18:16.436 --> 00:18:18.863
So that looks like
one of the mosques in town.

00:18:18.930 --> 00:18:22.390
I think it's a bit of
a hot button topic right now.

00:18:23.172 --> 00:18:25.705
And I'm not here to cause issues, but…

00:18:31.283 --> 00:18:32.116
How much?

00:18:32.787 --> 00:18:34.047
-Free, free man.
-Free?

00:18:34.807 --> 00:18:36.000
Thank you brother.

00:18:36.067 --> 00:18:37.908
-What's your name?
-Mohammed.

00:18:37.975 --> 00:18:39.636
-Assalamu alaikum.
-Wa alaikum salam, brother.

00:18:39.703 --> 00:18:41.183
Where are you from Mohammed?

00:18:41.250 --> 00:18:42.793
-Somalia.
-You like Maine?

00:18:42.860 --> 00:18:44.821
It's good. Good place.

00:18:44.888 --> 00:18:46.453
-You like Lewiston?
-Yeah.

00:18:46.520 --> 00:18:47.469
You like Lewiston?

00:18:47.536 --> 00:18:52.039
I've been traveling all the way down to
San Francisco, California, everywhere.

00:18:53.014 --> 00:18:54.313
Good people out there man.

00:18:54.380 --> 00:18:56.909
-So you've seen the country?
-Very nice, man.

00:18:57.813 --> 00:19:01.138
Every country
you see good people, bad people.

00:19:01.205 --> 00:19:03.710
-Yeah. It's called humans.
-Yep.

00:19:03.777 --> 00:19:05.343
-See you, man.
-Take care, brother.

00:19:12.733 --> 00:19:14.573
Why so much Red Bull in Lewiston?

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:17.329
Because it's the number one
beverage in Maine.

00:19:17.396 --> 00:19:18.318
-Are you serious?

00:19:18.385 --> 00:19:20.224
-Yeah. We sell more than water.

00:19:20.291 --> 00:19:21.572
-Come on.

00:19:21.639 --> 00:19:23.686
 Yeah, more than Poland Spring Water

00:19:23.753 --> 00:19:25.698
-More than Coca-Cola?
-Mm-hmm.

00:19:25.765 --> 00:19:28.203
-Get out of town. More than Monster?
-Yeah.

00:19:28.270 --> 00:19:29.994
-Red Bull represents.
-Yes.

00:19:30.061 --> 00:19:32.749
-You guys work in a team, huh?
-Yeah. [laughs]

00:19:32.816 --> 00:19:35.969
-So you sell a ton of this stuff here?
-Oh yeah. It's crazy.

00:19:36.036 --> 00:19:37.267
Sells like liquid crack.

00:19:37.334 --> 00:19:38.401
-Really?

00:19:38.468 --> 00:19:40.065
-Everyone's on it.
-Yeah.

00:19:40.132 --> 00:19:43.108
Everyone. Even the little kids.
That scares me a little bit.

00:19:43.175 --> 00:19:45.375
You might be on it.
You're pretty charged up.

00:19:45.442 --> 00:19:46.795
I had one this morning.

00:19:47.600 --> 00:19:49.129
I'm always this energized.

00:19:58.369 --> 00:20:00.625
How you doing, sir?
How's business these days?

00:20:00.692 --> 00:20:02.066
-Business is amazing.
-It's jamming?

00:20:02.133 --> 00:20:03.003
Oh. Hell yeah.

00:20:03.070 --> 00:20:05.146
-You're from Lewiston?
-Yeah. Born and raised.

00:20:05.213 --> 00:20:08.145
-You guys sell all this stuff legally
here in Maine, right?

00:20:08.212 --> 00:20:09.045
-Okay.

00:20:09.112 --> 00:20:11.245
-All with licenses.
-All with licenses?

00:20:11.312 --> 00:20:12.145
Yep.

00:20:12.212 --> 00:20:16.027
-So if someone wants to come in here
and get weed, like this new weed strain.

00:20:16.094 --> 00:20:17.513
-Just have an ID?
-That's it.

00:20:17.580 --> 00:20:20.309
And you don't open your bathroom
for a good reason.

00:20:20.376 --> 00:20:21.215
Yes.

00:20:21.282 --> 00:20:24.634
It's either needles,
break it, or all of the above.

00:20:25.190 --> 00:20:27.390
Downtown Lewiston,
there would be no toilets.

00:20:27.457 --> 00:20:30.285
If you find the toilets,
I would not recommend you use it.

00:20:30.352 --> 00:20:33.702
-I'm not going to find a toilet?
-You're not going to find anything.

00:20:33.769 --> 00:20:37.483
Unfortunately, that's the outcome
that is because of all the problems

00:20:37.550 --> 00:20:39.499
that's going on in downtown right now.

00:20:39.566 --> 00:20:40.599
-Okay.

00:20:40.666 --> 00:20:42.295
Red Bull sales. How are they?

00:20:42.362 --> 00:20:44.707
Red Bull, we are up 90% last year.

00:20:44.774 --> 00:20:46.099
What?! Why?!

00:20:46.866 --> 00:20:48.185
People love energy drinks.

00:20:48.252 --> 00:20:51.887
That's the fastest growing market
in the nation right now.

00:20:51.954 --> 00:20:54.141
Beer sales are down.

00:20:54.601 --> 00:20:57.190
Red Bull sales and Monster sales are up.

00:20:57.257 --> 00:20:59.642
How much is a Red Bull these days?

00:21:00.162 --> 00:21:04.495
It depends on the size, but it starts at
about three bucks to six.

00:21:05.633 --> 00:21:07.247
Take care, man. Have a good one.

00:21:07.314 --> 00:21:08.433
-Have a good day.

00:21:09.433 --> 00:21:10.701
[Peter] Take care. Thank you.

00:21:11.685 --> 00:21:12.792
That's wild.

00:21:12.859 --> 00:21:15.034
Red Bull sales up 90%.

00:21:16.740 --> 00:21:19.407
You guys are really nice in Maine
with the crosswalks.

00:21:19.474 --> 00:21:21.217
-Yeah.
-You let everyone go.

00:21:21.284 --> 00:21:22.740
-Yeah, man.
-Love it.

00:21:23.491 --> 00:21:26.103
Very good drivers here
I gotta say in Maine overall,

00:21:26.170 --> 00:21:28.961
it's so much less stressful
than most places.

00:21:29.028 --> 00:21:32.679
Okay. So we have a lot of
these apartment buildings,

00:21:32.746 --> 00:21:36.472
and so I'm going to give you
the information I'm pretty sure of,

00:21:36.539 --> 00:21:40.769
and I'm not going to speculate or tell you
things I really don't know are true.

00:21:40.836 --> 00:21:42.859
So since 2001,

00:21:42.926 --> 00:21:46.531
roughly 4,000 immigrants have come here

00:21:47.212 --> 00:21:48.365
mostly from Somalia.

00:21:48.432 --> 00:21:51.482
Now they didn't all just come
directly here from Somalia.

00:21:51.549 --> 00:21:55.322
What happens often,
and this is what I was told off camera

00:21:55.389 --> 00:21:56.919
is a family comes here,

00:21:57.611 --> 00:22:00.761
family members in another city
and they all want to be together.

00:22:00.828 --> 00:22:03.728
So Lewiston has been
one of those places that

00:22:04.172 --> 00:22:07.422
just attracts families and

00:22:08.449 --> 00:22:11.114
ethnicities that want to
be with one another.

00:22:11.181 --> 00:22:15.353
The federal government funds
the initial refugee settlement system,

00:22:15.420 --> 00:22:18.688
so if these were refugees back when
the Civil War

00:22:18.755 --> 00:22:21.314
was was hot and heavy in Somalia.

00:22:21.381 --> 00:22:22.281
They came over.

00:22:22.348 --> 00:22:25.062
The U.S. government funds refugees

00:22:25.129 --> 00:22:27.835
through the State Department's
refugee program.

00:22:28.508 --> 00:22:30.375
Okay. So then once approved,

00:22:31.042 --> 00:22:35.830
each refugee case is assigned
to a non-profit resettlement agency,

00:22:35.897 --> 00:22:40.030
such as Catholic Charities,
Church World Service, World Relief,

00:22:40.637 --> 00:22:44.415
Global Refuge, HIAS, IRC,

00:22:45.218 --> 00:22:47.165
USCRI.

00:22:47.232 --> 00:22:49.608
A lot of those.
I'm sure you guys don't know of,

00:22:49.675 --> 00:22:52.895
but Catholic Charities
is pretty visible across the country.

00:22:52.970 --> 00:22:57.700
The federal government
basically outsources to those NGOs,

00:22:57.767 --> 00:23:01.141
and then those NGOs disperse resources.

00:23:01.635 --> 00:23:04.223
How much gets dispersed?

00:23:04.685 --> 00:23:06.715
I don't have that number.

00:23:06.782 --> 00:23:08.603
And that's a real tough one to get.

00:23:08.670 --> 00:23:11.046
You can't just walk up
to someone and say, like,

00:23:11.113 --> 00:23:14.659
how much did you get,
and are you still receiving money?

00:23:14.726 --> 00:23:18.603
In some places I read,
it's very much a temporary thing,

00:23:19.137 --> 00:23:22.361
as in after a few months
and people are settled in,

00:23:22.428 --> 00:23:26.117
then there's no more real money for them.

00:23:26.184 --> 00:23:30.173
And then in other sources I've read,
there's child care,

00:23:30.240 --> 00:23:32.436
there's subsidized apartments,

00:23:33.176 --> 00:23:34.563
and all sorts of resources.

00:23:34.630 --> 00:23:36.297
It's really tough to know.

00:23:36.885 --> 00:23:39.723
And this is where I run to a line, guys.

00:23:39.790 --> 00:23:41.522
I'm not an investigative journalist.

00:23:41.589 --> 00:23:43.187
To really get this place,

00:23:43.254 --> 00:23:46.719
the story of Lewiston,
is to spend months on the ground here,

00:23:46.786 --> 00:23:49.428
and do hundreds of interviews,

00:23:49.495 --> 00:23:52.389
and really get to
the bottom of how things work,

00:23:52.456 --> 00:23:54.401
and the mechanics of how things work.

00:23:54.468 --> 00:23:56.503
And you might say, well, just do that.

00:23:56.570 --> 00:24:00.112
A, I don't want to be in Lewiston
for a few months.

00:24:00.179 --> 00:24:01.806
B, I'd kill the YouTube channel,

00:24:01.873 --> 00:24:04.402
'cause you guys
want to see constant content.

00:24:04.469 --> 00:24:07.083
You know, we do three videos a month,

00:24:07.150 --> 00:24:11.103
and the second I go on, you know,
two, three months on one story,

00:24:11.170 --> 00:24:13.016
then the channel dies.

00:24:13.539 --> 00:24:17.441
So we do need… I think that's one of
our big problems in this country.

00:24:17.508 --> 00:24:20.028
Is we need more investigative journalism.

00:24:20.095 --> 00:24:23.876
Establishments that have
the resources and the time

00:24:23.943 --> 00:24:28.053
to go deep on the stories
that really need a deep dig on.

00:24:28.120 --> 00:24:31.074
Because I can come in here
and make all sorts of accusations

00:24:31.141 --> 00:24:35.322
of what's going on or claims
and act like an authority.

00:24:35.389 --> 00:24:38.019
And I'm not at all with Lewiston.

00:24:38.532 --> 00:24:42.259
The goal is… You knew nothing
about Lewiston before this video.

00:24:42.326 --> 00:24:44.825
Now we got under the surface a little.

00:24:44.892 --> 00:24:46.893
There's a ton of digging, for sure.

00:24:46.960 --> 00:24:50.253
Look at that, though.
What a beautiful church.

00:24:50.320 --> 00:24:52.975
Basilica of Saints Peter and Paul.

00:24:53.042 --> 00:24:55.723
That has to be the nicest church in Maine,

00:24:55.790 --> 00:24:58.379
'cause I didn't see
anything like this in Portland.

00:24:58.446 --> 00:24:59.894
That is a beauty.

00:25:00.435 --> 00:25:02.231
I mean, to build that these days,

00:25:02.298 --> 00:25:06.164
that's in, I don't know,
tens and tens of millions.

00:25:06.810 --> 00:25:09.942
And back in those days
when mills were running

00:25:10.009 --> 00:25:14.845
and the money was coming through,
they just built beautiful architecture.

00:25:14.912 --> 00:25:17.333
Like what we see on the skyline here.

00:25:17.400 --> 00:25:19.080
-[car honking]
-[woman yells]

00:25:19.147 --> 00:25:21.800
-Unfortunately
we're not getting that back.

00:25:21.867 --> 00:25:24.033
We got someone yelling here.

00:25:24.100 --> 00:25:25.167
What's going on?

00:25:28.249 --> 00:25:29.515
-We watch you on YouTube!

00:25:29.582 --> 00:25:31.242
-Yes, are you from Lewiston?

00:25:31.309 --> 00:25:32.592
-We're from Auburn.

00:25:32.659 --> 00:25:34.992
-Tell me about it. What's going on?

00:25:35.059 --> 00:25:37.905
Well, in Auburn it's not too bad,

00:25:37.972 --> 00:25:40.224
but Lewiston
is going through a lot right now.

00:25:40.291 --> 00:25:43.191
-There's shootings, there's…
-Auburn's way better off, huh?

00:25:43.258 --> 00:25:46.173
Yep. And there's a lot of ICE
in Lewiston right now.

00:25:46.240 --> 00:25:48.932
And a lot of homeless in the park.

00:25:48.999 --> 00:25:52.218
I see… I walked out of my car
to a needle out of someone's arm.

00:25:52.285 --> 00:25:53.587
-Yes.
-It wasn't beautiful.

00:25:53.654 --> 00:25:57.206
I'm not trying to put Maine under the bus.
I'm trying to show all sides.

00:25:57.273 --> 00:25:59.681
And I'm showing this.
I mean, this is amazing.

00:25:59.748 --> 00:26:00.801
We actually go here.

00:26:00.868 --> 00:26:01.801
-You do?
-Yeah.

00:26:02.294 --> 00:26:03.791
What else should I see?

00:26:03.858 --> 00:26:07.058
I walked downtown.
I walked to this Italian deli.

00:26:07.139 --> 00:26:08.618
-Oh, the Italian bakery. Yeah.

00:26:08.685 --> 00:26:11.393
The churches are a big thing,
which you just saw.

00:26:11.460 --> 00:26:13.601
-Homeless in the park is…
-Yeah.

00:26:14.233 --> 00:26:16.383
The homeless in the park,
you should really…

00:26:16.450 --> 00:26:19.334
-Did you grow up here, ma'am?
-I grew up in Wyndham.

00:26:19.401 --> 00:26:21.906
-So, you saw what it was like
back in the day?

00:26:21.973 --> 00:26:24.379
Lisbon Street was all bars.
It was a happy place.

00:26:24.446 --> 00:26:26.100
-Oh.
-Restaurants, bars.

00:26:26.167 --> 00:26:30.105
So, would you say, in your lifespan,
Lewiston's at its worst point right now?

00:26:30.172 --> 00:26:32.872
-It's the worst I've seen it.
-It's not getting better?

00:26:32.939 --> 00:26:33.772
No.

00:26:34.513 --> 00:26:37.957
We actually had a friend
that was detained by ICE

00:26:38.024 --> 00:26:40.263
and all his charges were dismissed.

00:26:40.330 --> 00:26:42.416
He was trying to seek asylum.

00:26:42.483 --> 00:26:44.712
And he had a court date,
and we went with him

00:26:44.779 --> 00:26:47.029
because he doesn't
understand a lot of English

00:26:47.096 --> 00:26:49.867
and all his charges were dismissed.

00:26:49.934 --> 00:26:53.358
We stepped out of the courtroom
and he had ICE waiting for him

00:26:53.425 --> 00:26:54.809
the second he stepped out.

00:26:54.876 --> 00:26:56.833
So, there's a lot of that going on.

00:26:56.900 --> 00:26:59.752
-There's a lot of ICE in town right now?
-ICE. Yeah.

00:26:59.819 --> 00:27:02.323
-It comes in waves with ICE or no?
-No.

00:27:02.390 --> 00:27:04.266
Or you just never know?

00:27:04.333 --> 00:27:05.346
You never know.

00:27:05.413 --> 00:27:08.510
And now they're, you know,
disguising in vehicles.

00:27:08.577 --> 00:27:10.940
They're driving around regular cars now.

00:27:11.007 --> 00:27:15.670
And Maine has allowed them to start
putting license plates on the vehicles so,

00:27:15.737 --> 00:27:20.667
you know, if you are an immigrant
you can be standing outside your house,

00:27:20.734 --> 00:27:24.334
walk into the store
and they can grab you at any point.

00:27:25.294 --> 00:27:28.394
-Everyone's on board with the criminals
getting bounced, right?

00:27:28.461 --> 00:27:29.294
Yeah.

00:27:29.361 --> 00:27:32.336
It's the gray zone
that most of the country's on board with.

00:27:32.416 --> 00:27:36.172
Like, been here 10 years, paid taxes,
we got to figure out something, right?

00:27:36.239 --> 00:27:39.171
There's a major criminal element,
so I understand that part

00:27:39.238 --> 00:27:43.793
and I agree, but it's the other people
that get caught in between the crossfires.

00:27:43.860 --> 00:27:46.067
-Oh, I'm sorry.
I didn't come at a good time.

00:27:46.134 --> 00:27:49.800
No, but the homeless
is definitely, um, and the drugs.

00:27:50.320 --> 00:27:51.949
-You saw the needles.
-Yeah.

00:27:52.016 --> 00:27:53.673
-Is this your daughter?
-Yes.

00:27:53.740 --> 00:27:56.302
-Okay. Do you worry
about that in school? The drugs?

00:27:56.369 --> 00:28:00.854
Well, not really in school
but walking around town sometimes,

00:28:01.263 --> 00:28:04.133
you always have to watch yourself,

00:28:04.697 --> 00:28:07.932
especially being a girl,
a young girl walking around here,

00:28:07.999 --> 00:28:12.021
because, you know,
the homeless people, they're not all bad,

00:28:12.088 --> 00:28:15.217
but when they're high on meth,
crack, whatever.

00:28:15.284 --> 00:28:17.588
They're, you know,
they can do anything to you.

00:28:17.655 --> 00:28:19.806
So it's just a worry for them

00:28:19.873 --> 00:28:22.933
and you gotta worry about
yourself at the same time.

00:28:23.000 --> 00:28:24.750
-It was awesome to see you.
-Thanks.

00:28:24.817 --> 00:28:28.283
Sorry to go all dark on that,
but it came out and thank you guys.

00:28:28.350 --> 00:28:31.283
-Thank you for doing what you do.
-Thank you.

00:28:39.158 --> 00:28:41.285
Here we have Quiet City Books.

00:28:41.352 --> 00:28:44.688
Great time to mention, guys,
I'm going to be around the country

00:28:44.755 --> 00:28:47.901
at bookshops meeting you face to face.

00:28:47.968 --> 00:28:49.365
Early August.

00:28:49.432 --> 00:28:52.260
The book signing tour,
Your Fellow Americans.

00:28:52.947 --> 00:28:57.339
New York City, Boston, Greater Chicago,
Greater Detroit, Greater Phoenix,

00:28:57.406 --> 00:28:59.358
working on more locations.

00:28:59.425 --> 00:29:01.375
The publisher sets those up, not me,

00:29:01.442 --> 00:29:04.742
but I'm working hard
to get to more of the country.

00:29:04.809 --> 00:29:06.925
Would love to see you there.

00:29:06.992 --> 00:29:08.421
I got a QR code here.

00:29:08.488 --> 00:29:11.953
You can scan or link down below.

00:29:12.020 --> 00:29:15.950
Would love to see you in person
and give you a signed copy

00:29:16.017 --> 00:29:18.242
of Your Fellow Americans.

00:29:18.309 --> 00:29:21.449
It's all of our stories.
It's 10 stories in total.

00:29:21.516 --> 00:29:24.474
The cool thing about it is
you can pick it up, read a story,

00:29:24.541 --> 00:29:27.609
which doesn't take too long,
and then put it down.

00:29:27.676 --> 00:29:32.100
It doesn't take 500 pages
of your attention.

00:29:32.600 --> 00:29:34.495
We got some color up here.

00:29:34.562 --> 00:29:36.478
A lot of color on the walls.

00:29:36.545 --> 00:29:39.966
It's getting a little nicer
over here I would say, this part of town.

00:29:40.033 --> 00:29:43.267
Lewiston does provide
a certain type of excitement.

00:29:43.776 --> 00:29:48.464
I think it's the unexpectedness of it
that really makes it interesting.

00:29:58.504 --> 00:30:02.362
Here you see the Bates Mill,
one of Lewiston's many textile mills.

00:30:02.429 --> 00:30:06.632
"In 1852, the Bates Mill
started producing the cotton goods

00:30:06.699 --> 00:30:10.852
that led to its success
as Maine's largest employer."

00:30:10.919 --> 00:30:13.985
You can see it's all in French here,
down below.

00:30:14.789 --> 00:30:16.694
The story goes something like this.

00:30:16.761 --> 00:30:20.822
The Androscoggin River
powered the mills from the 1860s

00:30:21.249 --> 00:30:23.215
to the early 1900s.

00:30:23.282 --> 00:30:28.471
For generations this was one of Maine's
greatest working-class industrial cities.

00:30:28.539 --> 00:30:31.203
And guys, we're gonna go
past more of these buildings,

00:30:31.270 --> 00:30:34.559
but they go on for quite some time.
Huge buildings.

00:30:34.626 --> 00:30:37.660
The camera is not
really capturing them in full.

00:30:38.159 --> 00:30:41.710
But in '92, it closed because
the economics were just better overseas,

00:30:41.777 --> 00:30:45.304
like so much
of what's happened across America.

00:30:45.836 --> 00:30:48.632
And it put roughly
8,000 locals out of work

00:30:49.645 --> 00:30:50.478
at the time.

00:30:50.545 --> 00:30:52.830
And now the town is

00:30:52.897 --> 00:30:56.807
roughly 37,000 residents.

00:30:57.205 --> 00:30:59.715
I think at its peak,
it wasn't even that much more.

00:30:59.782 --> 00:31:04.384
I think it was like 45,000,
something like that.

00:31:04.947 --> 00:31:07.913
So when the mills closed
the population went down a bit.

00:31:07.980 --> 00:31:11.787
But then a lot of immigrants
came in right in the early 2000s.

00:31:11.854 --> 00:31:14.709
So that kept the population
somewhat afloat.

00:31:15.170 --> 00:31:17.868
But it looks like
they're doing something here.

00:31:17.935 --> 00:31:19.921
Bates Mill Complex.

00:31:20.819 --> 00:31:22.798
So there are a few businesses going on.

00:31:22.865 --> 00:31:26.659
I mean, buildings like this
are just absolutely beautiful.

00:31:26.726 --> 00:31:29.859
Look at the granite
above and below the windows.

00:31:30.229 --> 00:31:31.960
Just done in style.

00:31:33.378 --> 00:31:36.045
-Look at that thing.
-[car passes loudly]

00:31:36.975 --> 00:31:39.895
I don't even know what that is?
Never seen a car like that.

00:31:39.962 --> 00:31:41.818
But it just keeps on going and going.

00:31:41.885 --> 00:31:43.504
All of these mills.

00:31:43.571 --> 00:31:44.624
Street after street.

00:31:44.691 --> 00:31:47.557
I can't record all of it 'cause
I'll put you guys to sleep

00:31:47.624 --> 00:31:49.400
but it just continues on.

00:31:50.060 --> 00:31:52.424
Just think of all the workers,
all the activity.

00:31:52.491 --> 00:31:56.507
I'm sure the air quality was bad in town
and the work was rough.

00:31:56.574 --> 00:32:00.676
But social cohesion
as far as everyone having a job.

00:32:01.090 --> 00:32:04.757
I'm sure strong families,
school system, all that stuff.

00:32:05.509 --> 00:32:08.145
You know, there was probably
a thriving culture here.

00:32:08.212 --> 00:32:10.945
Everyone filling in the church on Sunday.

00:32:11.742 --> 00:32:14.737
And it's easy to romanticize
the old times.

00:32:15.801 --> 00:32:16.776
That was hard work.

00:32:16.843 --> 00:32:20.043
I don't think most people
would want to hack that out these days.

00:32:20.110 --> 00:32:23.710
But hey, if you know no different
then It's all normal.

00:32:25.478 --> 00:32:28.784
It's pretty much mill, church, home, bar.

00:32:31.780 --> 00:32:32.881
So you live in here?

00:32:32.948 --> 00:32:36.923
[man] Yep, it's awesome. I like it. It has
cathedral ceilings, nice big windows.

00:32:36.990 --> 00:32:38.010
What's rent like?

00:32:38.077 --> 00:32:42.560
It's more like for whatever you bring in
for your job and all that stuff.

00:32:42.627 --> 00:32:45.567
They just run all your pay stubs
and your bank account.

00:32:45.634 --> 00:32:47.150
They just base it off of that.

00:32:47.217 --> 00:32:49.838
So if you make too much,
that's what this place is.

00:32:49.905 --> 00:32:53.150
It's going to be an actual apartment.
So that'll be market cost.

00:32:53.230 --> 00:32:54.793
-This one, market cost.
-Yeah.

00:32:54.860 --> 00:32:58.524
But this one,
say you make $40,000 or $30,000.

00:32:58.591 --> 00:33:00.583
What is rent like, you think, roughly?

00:33:00.650 --> 00:33:02.975
It's probably $700 to $800
something like that.

00:33:03.042 --> 00:33:05.542
-Okay, so it's keeping people in town?
-Absolutely.

00:33:05.609 --> 00:33:09.412
Plus it's getting people off the streets
and actually thriving in their life.

00:33:09.479 --> 00:33:12.181
Actually getting good jobs,
able to get cars and stuff.

00:33:12.248 --> 00:33:14.463
-Can I go in with you?
-Yeah, if you want to check out.

00:33:14.530 --> 00:33:16.252
I mean, it looks good from the outside.

00:33:16.330 --> 00:33:19.760
I like how they're converting
the whole mill into something.

00:33:19.827 --> 00:33:20.815
Yeah.

00:33:20.882 --> 00:33:22.282
How are you, brother?

00:33:22.787 --> 00:33:24.448
-Tyler.
-What's going on?

00:33:24.515 --> 00:33:26.194
What's happening, my man?

00:33:26.261 --> 00:33:28.141
-How are you, brother?
-Good, brother.

00:33:28.208 --> 00:33:30.413
-Dude, I know you from somewhere.
-All right.

00:33:30.480 --> 00:33:32.391
-I'm Tyler's brother.
-You are?

00:33:32.458 --> 00:33:35.143
-Yeah, twin brother. Can't you tell?
-Are you on TikTok?

00:33:35.210 --> 00:33:36.816
I just aged worse than him.

00:33:36.883 --> 00:33:39.310
-You're just joshing me, right?
-I'm joshing you.

00:33:39.377 --> 00:33:41.529
-I'm Tyler-ing you.
-Dude, I know him.

00:33:41.596 --> 00:33:45.161
Dude, I swear I've seen your face,
though, on YouTube or TikTok.

00:33:45.228 --> 00:33:47.286
-Quite possibly, brother.
-Or something.

00:33:47.352 --> 00:33:49.463
-I go everywhere.
-What are you filming here?

00:33:49.530 --> 00:33:51.162
Doing a series on Maine.

00:33:51.229 --> 00:33:53.863
Just checking out
a bunch of cool places around here.

00:33:53.930 --> 00:33:57.173
I think this is cool.
You got some energy coming back into town.

00:33:57.669 --> 00:34:01.143
-A bunch of memorabilia stuff
that they kind of kept and restored.

00:34:01.210 --> 00:34:04.423
Like a bunch of cool pictures
of like what Lewiston used to be

00:34:04.490 --> 00:34:06.745
and like the Mills with the bird eye view.

00:34:06.813 --> 00:34:08.336
It just goes on and on and on.

00:34:08.403 --> 00:34:10.022
-Look at this, man.

00:34:10.880 --> 00:34:14.152
Views from the South Bridge
and all that good stuff.

00:34:14.219 --> 00:34:16.013
-So when did this come about?

00:34:16.080 --> 00:34:19.399
I want to say I've been here
for close to two years.

00:34:19.466 --> 00:34:23.707
It was around February 2026.

00:34:23.774 --> 00:34:25.878
I think it was like 2024.

00:34:25.945 --> 00:34:26.778
Okay.

00:34:26.845 --> 00:34:28.997
That's when we
finally released this place.

00:34:29.064 --> 00:34:31.135
-So are young people staying in town?

00:34:31.202 --> 00:34:32.820
-A little bit.
-Little bit?

00:34:32.887 --> 00:34:36.462
I don't really know too much about
any of the people that live here.

00:34:36.529 --> 00:34:38.611
Just a few that I kind of hang out with.

00:34:38.678 --> 00:34:39.953
-Okay.

00:34:40.020 --> 00:34:42.202
-Did you grow up here in Lewiston?
-Oh yeah.

00:34:42.269 --> 00:34:44.959
That's the green bridge to…

00:34:45.026 --> 00:34:48.000
-When you're walking towards…
-Oh yeah. How was it growing up here?

00:34:48.067 --> 00:34:48.900
It's sweet.

00:34:48.967 --> 00:34:52.635
I mean, it has its tips,
like substance abuse, homelessness,

00:34:52.720 --> 00:34:56.206
and all that good stuff which is not
really good but they're trying.

00:34:56.273 --> 00:35:00.051
If they bring more places like this
around town, then people

00:35:00.118 --> 00:35:02.834
actually will have a better chance.

00:35:02.901 --> 00:35:06.032
-Yeah. They just got to get off the drugs.
That's the hard part.

00:35:06.099 --> 00:35:06.932
It is.

00:35:07.000 --> 00:35:09.856
Can I keep going with you, Tyler?
You want to kick me out?

00:35:09.923 --> 00:35:11.513
-No, you're fine, man.
-All right.

00:35:11.580 --> 00:35:15.261
-So back in the day,
it was just a thriving place, huh?

00:35:15.328 --> 00:35:18.180
-Yeah. It's beautiful.
Look at the old time movie theater.

00:35:18.247 --> 00:35:19.258
-Oh, yeah.

00:35:19.325 --> 00:35:21.992
-I wish we could go back to those times.

00:35:22.563 --> 00:35:26.323
Life is just what you make it,
the way that you live, you know?

00:35:26.390 --> 00:35:28.690
So it could be rewarding
or it could be sh*tty.

00:35:28.757 --> 00:35:31.504
It really depends on
how you take care of it, you know?

00:35:31.571 --> 00:35:32.722
-How do you look at it?

00:35:32.789 --> 00:35:36.720
I look at the same energy
you put into yourself and your mind

00:35:36.787 --> 00:35:40.443
and just like the way you want to live
around you everything else matches.

00:35:40.510 --> 00:35:43.653
Your vibes attract your tribe.
That's one of my favorite quotes.

00:35:43.720 --> 00:35:45.886
-That's a good one. How old are you, Tyler?

00:35:45.953 --> 00:35:46.786
-28.
-Nice.

00:35:47.511 --> 00:35:49.825
-Where are you working?
-The Dough Company.

00:35:49.892 --> 00:35:52.805
It's a manufacturing for pizza dough
here in Maine.

00:35:52.872 --> 00:35:54.931
So I manufacture a whole bunch of dough.

00:35:54.998 --> 00:35:56.854
-Okay. I smelled that on the streets.

00:35:56.921 --> 00:35:58.783
-Yeah. This is my…

00:35:58.850 --> 00:36:00.334
Oh, this is great, man.

00:36:00.401 --> 00:36:02.223
Oh, this is awesome.

00:36:02.290 --> 00:36:03.130
High ceilings.

00:36:03.197 --> 00:36:05.197
Yeah. Cathedral ceilings, bro.

00:36:05.832 --> 00:36:06.956
What's going on here?

00:36:07.023 --> 00:36:08.572
Oh, it's my card streaming.

00:36:08.639 --> 00:36:10.760
I do card streams
for, like, trading cards.

00:36:10.827 --> 00:36:11.813
-No way.
-Yeah.

00:36:11.880 --> 00:36:13.408
I make some pretty good money.

00:36:13.475 --> 00:36:16.081
I think so far I made
close to $1,500 just this year

00:36:16.148 --> 00:36:18.629
and I started in March or May.

00:36:18.696 --> 00:36:19.644
-Okay.

00:36:19.711 --> 00:36:21.934
-On YouTube, or what are you doing?
-Whatnot.

00:36:22.001 --> 00:36:24.230
-Okay. Don't even know it.
-Whatnot's really sweet.

00:36:24.297 --> 00:36:26.235
-Whatnot? What's your account?

00:36:26.302 --> 00:36:30.423
-It's Tyturg420. T-Y-T-U-R-G 420.
-There you go.

00:36:30.933 --> 00:36:33.373
-Okay. So you live in here alone?
-Yeah.

00:36:33.440 --> 00:36:37.640
My bedroom's a little bit of a mess,
but it's still pretty nice.

00:36:38.749 --> 00:36:41.281
Got a whole bunch of stuff
just laying around, but…

00:36:41.348 --> 00:36:43.813
-Okay. And you do this
for $700 a month rent?

00:36:43.880 --> 00:36:44.880
This is like…

00:36:45.720 --> 00:36:48.244
-As of right now, it's been like $860.
-$860?

00:36:48.311 --> 00:36:53.273
I just got a new job, so I have to
resubmit my W-2s, get it authorized.

00:36:53.340 --> 00:36:56.024
Okay. So working
at the dough company, good job?

00:36:56.091 --> 00:36:59.091
Yeah. It's a sweet job.
It's just my boss, he's 73 years old.

00:36:59.158 --> 00:37:02.330
So he can get a little irritable.
He doesn't have much Patience.

00:37:02.397 --> 00:37:04.509
-But it's good pay?
-Yeah. It's decent pay.

00:37:04.576 --> 00:37:07.753
Right now, I'm making like $18.
Same as I did. I'm just waiting.

00:37:07.820 --> 00:37:12.436
I think after a month or two, I get
another raise, so that'll be $19 an hour.

00:37:12.503 --> 00:37:14.263
-Okay. And you like anime?

00:37:14.330 --> 00:37:15.410
I love anime.

00:37:15.477 --> 00:37:18.252
This is just like a little bit
of the stuff I've put up.

00:37:18.319 --> 00:37:20.272
Yu-Gi-Oh is my thing.

00:37:20.339 --> 00:37:23.689
That's great. You can pay your bills fine.
You live in a nice place.

00:37:23.756 --> 00:37:28.284
Hell yeah, man. This is my first ever
place so it's been an experience.

00:37:28.351 --> 00:37:30.475
-Right on. Thanks for the show.
-I got you.

00:37:30.542 --> 00:37:31.753
My pleasure.

00:37:31.820 --> 00:37:33.989
-Take care.
-You too, man. Nice to meet you.

00:37:34.056 --> 00:37:34.889
You too.

00:37:39.590 --> 00:37:44.293
And this is the mighty Androscoggin River
that made it all happen.

00:37:44.360 --> 00:37:48.845
Look at the volume of water being pushed,
powering all the mills.

00:37:48.912 --> 00:37:52.863
It's what created the town
in this modern day iteration.

00:37:53.500 --> 00:37:56.033
We're going to go up the tree streets,

00:37:56.962 --> 00:37:58.795
like the guys said in the beginning.

00:37:58.862 --> 00:38:00.991
We touched on them a bit,
but go further in

00:38:01.058 --> 00:38:04.463
'cause that's supposedly
the toughest part of town.

00:38:04.530 --> 00:38:07.950
And then we're going to
flip the table completely

00:38:08.017 --> 00:38:12.720
and show you a very expensive
liberal arts college that's…

00:38:12.787 --> 00:38:16.550
I don't know if we can get on campus
but it's right next to downtown.

00:38:17.640 --> 00:38:21.300
$74,000 to go there.

00:38:21.367 --> 00:38:26.718
I think with housing and board,
it's something like $94,000.

00:38:27.210 --> 00:38:31.187
Endowment of a half billion dollars.

00:38:31.847 --> 00:38:34.468
And I forget the student count.
I'll pull that up.

00:38:34.535 --> 00:38:37.387
But there aren't that many students
going there either.

00:38:37.454 --> 00:38:41.708
But one of the… I don't know if it's
top tier, but it's pretty well known.

00:38:41.775 --> 00:38:44.428
Bates College. I've heard of it before.

00:38:44.495 --> 00:38:46.129
Hard to believe it's here.

00:38:46.620 --> 00:38:50.393
Here we have the Franco Center
Performing Arts and Events.

00:38:50.460 --> 00:38:55.207
"1860, 1930, Thousands of
French Canadians came to Lewiston area.

00:38:55.274 --> 00:38:58.894
Most by railroad from the farms
and villages of Quebec.

00:38:59.303 --> 00:39:04.338
They brought with them their language,
Catholic religion, cultural traditions,

00:39:04.405 --> 00:39:05.872
and a strong work ethic.

00:39:05.939 --> 00:39:08.311
And transplanted those values here.

00:39:08.378 --> 00:39:11.230
This monument is dedicated
by their descendants

00:39:11.297 --> 00:39:13.697
to the spirit of faith and optimism.

00:39:14.197 --> 00:39:15.730
Which they left to us."

00:39:17.206 --> 00:39:20.952
Yeah, so it's starting to feel
a little more French Canadian over here.

00:39:21.019 --> 00:39:22.787
This is called Little Canada.

00:39:23.330 --> 00:39:26.285
And a lot of the descendants
of those immigrants

00:39:26.352 --> 00:39:30.345
that came here back in the day
live on these streets.

00:39:30.412 --> 00:39:31.560
So they say.

00:39:32.804 --> 00:39:37.209
I walked into a super smoky bar
and the locals told me that.

00:39:37.962 --> 00:39:41.727
Also walked into a shop
where an Iranian guy works.

00:39:41.794 --> 00:39:43.779
Great guy. Iranian Jew.

00:39:43.846 --> 00:39:47.857
A lot of people don't know that.
There are a lot of Iranian Jews.

00:39:47.924 --> 00:39:50.916
He moved from Los Angeles to Lewiston.

00:39:51.569 --> 00:39:52.420
He likes it.

00:39:52.487 --> 00:39:55.811
He's like,
"Not everyone's trying to rip me off

00:39:55.878 --> 00:39:58.478
and people are nicer here in Lewiston."

00:39:59.105 --> 00:40:01.269
I was like wow, LA to here.

00:40:02.216 --> 00:40:03.563
How you guys doing?

00:40:03.630 --> 00:40:05.733
What's the Lewiston story?
What's going on?

00:40:05.800 --> 00:40:08.382
Lewiston is not a great place to live.

00:40:08.449 --> 00:40:09.961
It's a lot of shootings.

00:40:10.028 --> 00:40:12.266
It's a lot of rebellient kids.

00:40:12.333 --> 00:40:15.100
It's a lot of kids
that just come to the country

00:40:15.167 --> 00:40:18.236
and want to be gangsters
type of stuff. You know.

00:40:18.303 --> 00:40:21.944
If you ever wanted to come to Maine area.

00:40:22.011 --> 00:40:23.842
I would recommend going to Auburn.

00:40:23.909 --> 00:40:25.823
-It's a more chill place.
-Okay.

00:40:25.890 --> 00:40:28.262
There's always like fighting
between each other.

00:40:28.329 --> 00:40:30.111
Even for the same groups and stuff.

00:40:30.178 --> 00:40:32.489
Kids from immigrants,
people who are from here.

00:40:32.556 --> 00:40:33.889
Just like everybody.

00:40:34.361 --> 00:40:37.589
-So there's a lot of I don't want to say
gangs, but cliques here?

00:40:37.656 --> 00:40:40.975
-Different groups?
-There is definitely gangs now.

00:40:41.042 --> 00:40:41.875
-Okay.

00:40:43.090 --> 00:40:46.435
Back then there wasn't a lot
but now it's starting to be like

00:40:46.502 --> 00:40:48.907
a common thing that kids…

00:40:48.974 --> 00:40:51.178
-It's like a trend.
-Try to get into a gang.

00:40:51.245 --> 00:40:52.713
-It's a trend?
-Yeah.

00:40:52.780 --> 00:40:57.351
There was a shooting recently
with 12 year old kids.

00:40:58.404 --> 00:41:02.275
Like 10 or 11 of them
that had went to juvenile.

00:41:02.342 --> 00:41:03.671
-Oh geez.
-Yeah.

00:41:03.738 --> 00:41:06.388
For running around each other
and shooting each other.

00:41:06.455 --> 00:41:07.430
-Man.
-Yeah.

00:41:07.497 --> 00:41:09.232
It's pretty rough out here.

00:41:09.299 --> 00:41:11.803
-How do you guys stay out of trouble?
-I mean.

00:41:11.870 --> 00:41:13.483
You drive the cool car?

00:41:13.550 --> 00:41:16.843
Just drive the cool cars, man,
play sports. You know.

00:41:16.910 --> 00:41:19.722
-Sports is good for that, yeah.
-Play sports.

00:41:19.789 --> 00:41:22.843
We come from a very disciplined family.

00:41:22.910 --> 00:41:25.089
-Good family structure at home?
-Yes sir.

00:41:25.156 --> 00:41:28.055
Right on. You're saying sir even.
That's cool.

00:41:28.122 --> 00:41:30.710
-Sir.
-That's not even a Northeast thing to do.

00:41:30.777 --> 00:41:32.878
Very well educated. You know.

00:41:32.945 --> 00:41:36.233
-Cool. Do you guys want to stay in town
or bounce once you can?

00:41:36.300 --> 00:41:38.736
-We'll bounce once we can.
-Definitely.

00:41:38.803 --> 00:41:39.893
Okay.

00:41:39.960 --> 00:41:42.560
So. So if you don't have
a father figure in the home,

00:41:42.627 --> 00:41:44.427
I've been told this a million times.

00:41:44.494 --> 00:41:46.294
It's much easier to get into a gang.

00:41:46.361 --> 00:41:48.293
-Right?
-Yeah. It is. Definitely.

00:41:48.360 --> 00:41:50.819
That sh*t's not gonna fly
with your dad obviously.

00:41:50.886 --> 00:41:52.997
-Definitely not.
-My dad would spank me.

00:41:53.064 --> 00:41:54.855
My dad would definitely spank me.

00:41:54.922 --> 00:41:57.656
Yeah. We're following a straight line man.

00:41:58.110 --> 00:42:01.887
-I got the cousins giggling behind me.
I'm not going to put them on camera.

00:42:01.954 --> 00:42:03.887
You don't want to be on camera. Right?

00:42:03.954 --> 00:42:04.981
-[girl] No.
-Okay. Okay.

00:42:05.048 --> 00:42:08.007
You guys are gonna drop me off
in the roughest part of town.

00:42:08.074 --> 00:42:10.356
It's definitely the roughest part of town.

00:42:10.423 --> 00:42:11.822
-Okay.
-Yeah.

00:42:11.889 --> 00:42:14.318
Can I go shotgun 'cause I got the camera?

00:42:14.730 --> 00:42:15.863
Yes, sir.

00:42:19.575 --> 00:42:21.404
You keep a clean ride here, man.

00:42:21.471 --> 00:42:22.304
All the time.

00:42:22.371 --> 00:42:24.103
-You love your car.
-I love my car.

00:42:24.170 --> 00:42:26.631
Now you ladies are in on the camera.
You know that.

00:42:26.698 --> 00:42:27.812
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:42:27.879 --> 00:42:29.943
-I mean, in the background.
-Okay.

00:42:30.010 --> 00:42:32.005
Okay. What are your guys' names?

00:42:32.072 --> 00:42:33.224
My name is FC.

00:42:33.291 --> 00:42:34.225
-FC?
-Yeah.

00:42:34.292 --> 00:42:35.724
-Joel.
-Joel?

00:42:35.791 --> 00:42:37.587
-Ana Maria.
-Ana Maria.

00:42:37.654 --> 00:42:39.323
-Elian.
-Elian, Peter.

00:42:39.390 --> 00:42:40.563
What grade are you in?

00:42:40.630 --> 00:42:44.406
-I'm a freshman in college right now.
-Freshman in college? What college?

00:42:44.473 --> 00:42:46.338
-I go to CN.
-How is it?

00:42:47.164 --> 00:42:50.458
It's okay. It's a community college
we have in Auburn.

00:42:50.525 --> 00:42:52.872
So we've got
a lot of diversity over there.

00:42:52.939 --> 00:42:54.680
-I went to community college too.

00:42:54.747 --> 00:42:55.890
-Yeah?
-Yeah.

00:42:55.957 --> 00:42:59.174
So I did that for the two years
and then transfer to a four-year.

00:42:59.241 --> 00:43:01.781
That's what I did. What are you studying?

00:43:01.848 --> 00:43:03.662
-Criminal justice.
-Nice.

00:43:03.729 --> 00:43:06.194
What do you think about that right now?

00:43:06.261 --> 00:43:11.000
Right now I feel like people
might have different feelings about it

00:43:11.067 --> 00:43:15.642
because how they think about I want to
become a cop and stuff like that.

00:43:17.182 --> 00:43:20.464
People don't really
mess with the cops right now over here.

00:43:20.531 --> 00:43:22.555
-They don't mess with the cops here?
-No.

00:43:22.622 --> 00:43:25.197
But you've got to realize
that not all cops are bad.

00:43:25.264 --> 00:43:30.252
I believe that not all cops are bad,
but people don't always go for that here.

00:43:30.319 --> 00:43:32.485
Everybody gonna have their own opinion.

00:43:32.552 --> 00:43:35.216
-Yeah, I hear you.
Are we gramming back there?

00:43:35.283 --> 00:43:36.479
Yes! [giggling]

00:43:36.546 --> 00:43:39.598
-And then we've got Knox Street.
-We're recording a mini-vlog.

00:43:39.665 --> 00:43:42.045
-All right.
-Like, right now it's calm, right?

00:43:42.112 --> 00:43:42.945
-Okay.

00:43:43.012 --> 00:43:46.499
But on this same exact street,
it happens, lot of things,

00:43:46.566 --> 00:43:49.162
like a lot of shootings
and fighting right here.

00:43:49.229 --> 00:43:51.029
-This is the rough part of Lewiston?

00:43:51.096 --> 00:43:53.664
-This is sort of the rough part,
Knox Street.

00:43:53.731 --> 00:43:57.163
I guess you can call this like a block.
This is like a different block.

00:43:57.230 --> 00:43:58.641
Right now it's calm.

00:43:58.708 --> 00:44:01.808
I don't know if you're gon' find
people to interview and stuff,

00:44:01.875 --> 00:44:05.395
but I'm not going this is
the middle part of the worst side.

00:44:05.462 --> 00:44:06.897
-Thanks guys.
-I got you, bro.

00:44:06.964 --> 00:44:09.028
-Have a good day.
-Thank you.

00:44:09.095 --> 00:44:12.558
-It was nice meeting you. [giggles]
-High five. There we go.

00:44:12.625 --> 00:44:15.588
-Guys best of luck with everything.
-Thank you. You too.

00:44:15.655 --> 00:44:17.376
You know what's the beauty of your ages?

00:44:17.443 --> 00:44:19.183
You get everything ahead of you.

00:44:19.250 --> 00:44:21.317
-Go make the life you want.
-Yes sir.

00:44:21.384 --> 00:44:24.080
Just make sure you ask everybody
before recording anything.

00:44:24.147 --> 00:44:26.306
-I always do.
-Yeah okay.

00:44:26.373 --> 00:44:30.985
-No but even the streets they don't like.
-Oh here, yeah, yeah. No I always do.

00:44:31.052 --> 00:44:32.420
-All right guys.
-Be safe.

00:44:32.487 --> 00:44:33.820
All right take care.

00:44:37.423 --> 00:44:38.423
Cool kids.

00:44:39.701 --> 00:44:41.896
All right guys,
so part of the Lewiston story

00:44:41.963 --> 00:44:43.802
is a crime story here in Maine

00:44:43.870 --> 00:44:46.892
so I'm gonna
drop some facts for you here.

00:44:47.370 --> 00:44:51.503
So why does it have
such a rough reputation?

00:44:52.115 --> 00:44:56.728
Well the homicides actually the last
couple years aren't that high at all.

00:44:56.795 --> 00:45:00.157
It's been one last year,
one the year before,

00:45:00.224 --> 00:45:03.096
and so for having 37,000 people,

00:45:04.049 --> 00:45:06.546
that falls under the national average.

00:45:06.613 --> 00:45:07.446
[car honks]

00:45:07.513 --> 00:45:08.913
Now a few years back…

00:45:09.521 --> 00:45:10.521
What's up dude?

00:45:10.863 --> 00:45:12.229
[man speaking inaudibly]

00:45:12.296 --> 00:45:13.129
Get in.

00:45:14.157 --> 00:45:16.709
[man speaking inaudibly]

00:45:16.776 --> 00:45:17.609
Thank you.

00:45:20.672 --> 00:45:24.240
So national average
is actually lower, believe it or not.

00:45:24.805 --> 00:45:27.826
But in '23 there was a mass shooting here.

00:45:27.893 --> 00:45:29.345
Two locations.

00:45:29.412 --> 00:45:31.313
18 were killed.

00:45:31.380 --> 00:45:33.146
The biggest mass shooting in Maine.

00:45:33.213 --> 00:45:34.856
I forget how many injured.

00:45:34.923 --> 00:45:38.161
And so you know, it wasn't that long ago.

00:45:38.860 --> 00:45:43.973
Huge, huge, huge heavy event
for Lewiston and all of Maine actually.

00:45:44.040 --> 00:45:45.773
Biggest shooting in the state's history.

00:45:45.840 --> 00:45:47.049
It's known for that too.

00:45:47.116 --> 00:45:50.930
So that year the crime as far as homicides

00:45:50.997 --> 00:45:54.319
fell between Memphis and St. Louis.

00:45:54.386 --> 00:45:55.661
So it was very high.

00:45:55.728 --> 00:45:59.658
But it's a small town so just
a few numbers really tick things up.

00:45:59.725 --> 00:46:01.168
But the last couple years

00:46:01.815 --> 00:46:04.873
homicides are roughly low.

00:46:04.940 --> 00:46:09.188
Now homicides are easy numbers
because they're quite objective.

00:46:09.935 --> 00:46:12.335
The other crime is tougher to track.

00:46:12.789 --> 00:46:16.294
So a lot of stuff doesn't get reported.

00:46:16.361 --> 00:46:18.936
Depending on jurisdiction
something can be a felony.

00:46:19.003 --> 00:46:20.992
Something can be a misdemeanor.

00:46:21.059 --> 00:46:25.494
But in 2024 Lewiston police responded to

00:46:26.147 --> 00:46:29.018
53,038 calls for service.

00:46:29.662 --> 00:46:33.330
Opened 5,234 cases

00:46:33.936 --> 00:46:38.368
and made 2,732 arrests.

00:46:39.005 --> 00:46:42.625
Which is a lot for a population of 37,000.

00:46:43.537 --> 00:46:44.495
So there you go.

00:46:44.562 --> 00:46:47.738
I hope that clears things up
a little bit here with the crime.

00:46:47.805 --> 00:46:50.295
And then there's always
quality of life stuff.

00:46:50.362 --> 00:46:54.591
You know, there's the stuff
that you don't see on a spreadsheet.

00:46:54.658 --> 00:46:58.125
That being the person,
you know, yelling at the wall,

00:46:59.032 --> 00:47:01.450
throwing things at you on the street.

00:47:01.517 --> 00:47:03.063
Just disturbances.

00:47:03.889 --> 00:47:08.622
And quite often you just can't
put a metric on that sort of stuff.

00:47:08.689 --> 00:47:11.928
Like, you can feel
an uneasiness in a place.

00:47:11.995 --> 00:47:12.942
And I'm sure

00:47:14.268 --> 00:47:16.375
especially at night, day is always better,

00:47:16.442 --> 00:47:20.509
Lewiston has a fair amount of that
on these streets back here.

00:47:27.811 --> 00:47:31.304
Look at these prices here, $5.63, $5.51.

00:47:31.850 --> 00:47:34.953
2.42 pounds, $4.82.

00:47:35.020 --> 00:47:35.983
Wow.

00:47:36.512 --> 00:47:38.854
Pork chops, $6.62.

00:47:39.660 --> 00:47:42.193
Even these steaks, good price, $13.34.

00:47:43.361 --> 00:47:46.793
-So Susan, you grew up here?
-I did.

00:47:46.860 --> 00:47:50.690
I grew up probably
five blocks down, past Kennedy Park.

00:47:50.757 --> 00:47:53.075
-So now you own the store.
-I own the store.

00:47:53.142 --> 00:47:55.921
Okay, so what's going on in Lewiston?
What's your take?

00:47:55.988 --> 00:47:58.347
I mean, there's a lot of great things
going on in Lewiston.

00:47:58.414 --> 00:48:02.298
But I'm sure you're hearing
more of the sour thing.

00:48:02.365 --> 00:48:05.046
No, it's interesting.
I'm doing a Maine series

00:48:05.113 --> 00:48:07.801
and talked to Mainers,
a bunch of Mainers beforehand.

00:48:07.868 --> 00:48:11.635
When I mentioned Lewiston,
they said it's an absolute war zone

00:48:12.110 --> 00:48:14.137
or it's a pretty great place.

00:48:14.204 --> 00:48:15.890
Like there was no middle ground.

00:48:15.957 --> 00:48:19.338
It's interesting because when you hear
about Portland though, right?

00:48:19.405 --> 00:48:22.455
Just to bring up Portland,
you hear of all these great things.

00:48:22.522 --> 00:48:24.384
So everyone should go to Portland.

00:48:24.451 --> 00:48:26.903
But Portland actually has
more crime than Lewiston.

00:48:26.970 --> 00:48:31.624
We are experiencing
a lot of underage kids…

00:48:31.691 --> 00:48:32.729
Okay.

00:48:32.796 --> 00:48:34.713
…somehow getting a hold of these guns.

00:48:34.780 --> 00:48:37.704
And rather than having like your

00:48:38.176 --> 00:48:42.240
normal old school fist fight,
they're shooting at each other.

00:48:42.834 --> 00:48:44.939
It's very, very disturbing.

00:48:45.006 --> 00:48:47.281
Yeah. When did it get like that?

00:48:47.348 --> 00:48:50.290
It's gotten really bad
over the last five years.

00:48:50.357 --> 00:48:52.070
-And progressively gotten worse.
-Okay.

00:48:52.137 --> 00:48:54.100
Are there many services for kids?

00:48:54.167 --> 00:48:56.094
Are there many services in town?

00:48:56.161 --> 00:48:57.774
I think there are.

00:48:57.841 --> 00:49:02.279
You'll hear a lot of people that will say
we need more services.

00:49:02.346 --> 00:49:05.920
But I think it comes down to
we need accountability.

00:49:05.987 --> 00:49:09.387
And we need more parents,
active parents, parenting.

00:49:10.190 --> 00:49:11.601
Yeah, I just met these kids.

00:49:11.668 --> 00:49:14.118
They were talking about
how tough their father was

00:49:14.185 --> 00:49:16.485
and he doesn't let them
get away with anything.

00:49:16.552 --> 00:49:19.231
They were saying sir to me
like I was in the South.

00:49:19.298 --> 00:49:21.514
But you could tell
they were brought up well.

00:49:21.581 --> 00:49:25.020
And they're like we hate the crime here.
We don't associate with it.

00:49:25.087 --> 00:49:29.147
And then of course
I'm all over the country so common story,

00:49:29.214 --> 00:49:30.814
without a father figure,

00:49:31.372 --> 00:49:33.876
it's much more inviting
to get into a gang.

00:49:33.943 --> 00:49:37.327
Because that's that father figure, right?
That's that acceptance.

00:49:37.394 --> 00:49:40.244
I'm not sure that kids
actually go out looking for a gang.

00:49:40.311 --> 00:49:43.166
But I think what happens
is you have those adults

00:49:43.233 --> 00:49:46.958
that see a vulnerable situation
or a vulnerable kid.

00:49:47.025 --> 00:49:50.092
-So they kind of steer them in.
-Yeah.

00:49:50.159 --> 00:49:53.178
"We can do this for you."
And that's definitely happening.

00:49:53.245 --> 00:49:56.997
So they can have them
running their drugs, running their guns.

00:49:57.064 --> 00:49:59.212
-Because they're juveniles, right?
-Yeah.

00:49:59.279 --> 00:50:01.870
And unfortunately the juvenile system

00:50:02.496 --> 00:50:06.543
has not really done us in Maine
any justice anyway.

00:50:07.009 --> 00:50:10.075
A kid would get in trouble
for gun violence

00:50:10.142 --> 00:50:12.259
and be released to their parents.

00:50:12.326 --> 00:50:13.543
-Okay.
-That's a problem.

00:50:13.610 --> 00:50:17.383
-Oh, they would get caught shooting a gun
and get released to their parents?

00:50:17.450 --> 00:50:18.573
Oh, yes. Released to the parents.

00:50:18.640 --> 00:50:20.558
That's been going on
for the last few years.

00:50:20.625 --> 00:50:21.458
-Come on.

00:50:21.525 --> 00:50:24.639
So what happens is
if there's no accountability,

00:50:24.706 --> 00:50:26.485
you're going to continue to do it.

00:50:26.552 --> 00:50:29.477
-And it's only going to get worse.
-Basic human psychology.

00:50:29.544 --> 00:50:33.125
Yes. And that, I believe,
is why we are where we are today.

00:50:33.192 --> 00:50:34.475
-Here in Lewiston.
-Okay.

00:50:34.542 --> 00:50:36.142
Is that changing at all?

00:50:38.177 --> 00:50:42.633
I think we have seen a shift
over the last few months.

00:50:42.700 --> 00:50:47.803
Our police department
feels like they have more support.

00:50:48.234 --> 00:50:49.067
-Okay.

00:50:49.134 --> 00:50:53.351
With our city administrator,
city council, the DA's office.

00:50:54.036 --> 00:50:56.585
You know, the police
can only do so much, right?

00:50:56.652 --> 00:50:58.201
-The DA is very important.

00:50:58.268 --> 00:51:00.897
-Very. And the judges.
-And the judges.

00:51:00.964 --> 00:51:03.968
Because if the DA's
let everyone off easily,

00:51:04.796 --> 00:51:08.679
the police,
they put themselves in the problem.

00:51:08.746 --> 00:51:10.709
-That's right.
-Put their lives at risk.

00:51:10.776 --> 00:51:12.761
Then if they see that
person out the next day,

00:51:12.828 --> 00:51:14.604
they're like, what am I doing this for?

00:51:14.670 --> 00:51:17.052
-And that's how you break it down.
-Absolutely.

00:51:17.119 --> 00:51:19.346
I dealt with that firsthand
in San Francisco.

00:51:19.413 --> 00:51:21.263
-We had a real weak DA.
-Yep.

00:51:21.330 --> 00:51:23.162
-And a real weak mayor.
-Yeah.

00:51:23.229 --> 00:51:26.754
And it got so bad, DA was recalled.

00:51:26.821 --> 00:51:27.893
-Yeah.
-New mayor.

00:51:27.960 --> 00:51:30.601
So we have a great DA. I will say that.

00:51:30.668 --> 00:51:31.715
Okay, that's good.

00:51:31.782 --> 00:51:33.850
[man] So sorry, guys.
Just squeeze behind.

00:51:33.917 --> 00:51:35.517
Go for it. Squeeze away.

00:51:36.036 --> 00:51:39.162
I do think, unfortunately,
COVID played a big part,

00:51:39.229 --> 00:51:42.945
as much as I hate saying that 'cause
so many people use that for an excuse.

00:51:43.012 --> 00:51:47.164
But, you know, during that time,
really the judges and the jail said,

00:51:47.231 --> 00:51:51.083
we're not prosecuting anyone
unless it's like a really bad crime.

00:51:51.150 --> 00:51:51.983
Okay.

00:51:52.056 --> 00:51:57.621
So what happens is if you continue
to let people get away with small crimes,

00:51:57.688 --> 00:51:58.998
eventually it gets bigger.

00:51:59.065 --> 00:52:00.738
So I think that played a part in it too.

00:52:00.805 --> 00:52:01.783
Okay.

00:52:01.850 --> 00:52:04.775
-I see some development down there.
-Yes.

00:52:04.842 --> 00:52:08.809
That building, someone in the park
said you were part of that.

00:52:08.876 --> 00:52:10.377
Yes. What's that story?

00:52:10.444 --> 00:52:13.573
I'll be opening a grocery store
right there on the bottom.

00:52:13.640 --> 00:52:14.880
-Oh, cool.
-Yes.

00:52:14.947 --> 00:52:17.954
-But the building's not yours?
-The building is not mine, no.

00:52:18.021 --> 00:52:20.150
Who's moving in there? What is that story?

00:52:20.217 --> 00:52:22.673
So that is one and two bedrooms.

00:52:22.740 --> 00:52:24.567
Will these be low rent or high rent?

00:52:24.634 --> 00:52:27.523
So there'll be a mixture.
I think that's very important.

00:52:27.590 --> 00:52:32.647
You don't want to just keep low income
people kind of in a certain area.

00:52:32.714 --> 00:52:33.547
-Yeah.

00:52:33.614 --> 00:52:36.053
So I think it's important
that you do mixed use.

00:52:36.120 --> 00:52:39.391
What should people know about Lewiston?
What do they not know?

00:52:39.458 --> 00:52:41.208
Because it gets a bad rap in Maine.

00:52:41.275 --> 00:52:43.767
-Yeah.
-The rest of the country doesn't know.

00:52:43.835 --> 00:52:47.784
I think we're a service sector,
that's why it gets such a bad rap, right?

00:52:47.851 --> 00:52:50.036
We service so many people.

00:52:50.103 --> 00:52:53.658
If someone has an issue,
they get dumped off to Lewiston.

00:52:53.725 --> 00:52:56.304
But the great thing is,
is most of the time we help.

00:52:56.371 --> 00:52:57.363
-Okay.
-Okay?

00:52:57.430 --> 00:53:01.357
We're not just shooing people away,
but Lewiston, we're so resilient.

00:53:01.424 --> 00:53:03.240
The people are so resilient.

00:53:03.307 --> 00:53:06.320
And I'll just share that,
you know, in this area

00:53:06.387 --> 00:53:09.855
of Lewiston is
the most poverty-stricken area in Maine.

00:53:10.375 --> 00:53:11.723
-Right here?
-Right here.

00:53:11.790 --> 00:53:15.947
When someone is in need,
everyone pulls together to help.

00:53:16.014 --> 00:53:17.215
It is amazing.

00:53:17.282 --> 00:53:22.549
This little credit union here, when they
have a little drive for single mothers,

00:53:23.049 --> 00:53:26.361
it is the poorest people
at the beginning of the month

00:53:26.428 --> 00:53:28.396
that are giving the most money.

00:53:28.463 --> 00:53:29.643
It's very interesting.

00:53:29.710 --> 00:53:31.211
You know, I grew up here.

00:53:31.278 --> 00:53:34.474
I even caused trouble here,
if I was being honest.

00:53:34.541 --> 00:53:36.057
I brought my children up here.

00:53:36.124 --> 00:53:39.625
I have asked my children
to either live or invest here.

00:53:40.194 --> 00:53:42.383
Are you of Canadian descent?

00:53:42.450 --> 00:53:43.633
I am Mexican.

00:53:43.700 --> 00:53:44.553
-Oh!

00:53:44.620 --> 00:53:47.316
-Mexicans came here too?
-Yeah, some. My mother did.

00:53:47.383 --> 00:53:48.493
-Okay, cool.
-Yeah.

00:53:48.560 --> 00:53:53.173
So this building here across the street,
I purchased like four years ago.

00:53:53.240 --> 00:53:55.033
I completely rehabbed it.

00:53:55.100 --> 00:53:57.109
It was absolutely horrible.

00:53:57.176 --> 00:53:59.576
And I did the same thing
to that red house there.

00:53:59.643 --> 00:54:00.476
-Okay, nice.

00:54:01.363 --> 00:54:03.975
-Selling the store.
-Oh, is he the one who bought it?

00:54:04.042 --> 00:54:06.162
Oh no, he's going to make it
into a bong place.

00:54:06.229 --> 00:54:08.000
That's what everybody said.

00:54:08.067 --> 00:54:09.559
He's going to ruin it.

00:54:09.626 --> 00:54:13.444
We're moving down the street
into a large space.

00:54:13.511 --> 00:54:15.676
-We're going to have a grocery store.
-Oh.

00:54:15.743 --> 00:54:18.361
So you're selling this one,
you're moving down there.

00:54:18.428 --> 00:54:20.437
-I got my own apartment yesterday.
-Good!

00:54:20.504 --> 00:54:24.403
-Yeah, I'm working at the recycling place.
-Awesome!

00:54:24.470 --> 00:54:28.198
-You know how they tear buildings down?
-Yes. Oh, wonderful.

00:54:28.265 --> 00:54:29.132
Good for you.

00:54:29.200 --> 00:54:31.985
-You worry this turns into a bong place?
-Yeah.

00:54:32.052 --> 00:54:33.452
Is that what happens?

00:54:33.519 --> 00:54:38.333
Yeah, 'cause all the stores
that their family owns is all bongs.

00:54:39.019 --> 00:54:41.619
You know, they don't keep
maintaining grocery stores.

00:54:41.686 --> 00:54:45.553
They might have some food
but I bet you our fresh produce goes away.

00:54:45.620 --> 00:54:48.663
-Yeah. It's too bad.
-Patels that are coming in.

00:54:48.730 --> 00:54:49.783
Yeah.

00:54:49.850 --> 00:54:51.141
-Patels?
-Right.

00:54:51.208 --> 00:54:54.023
-Are you serious?
-They're down every corner.

00:54:54.090 --> 00:54:55.463
They bought every store up here.

00:54:55.530 --> 00:54:58.296
-And you sold it to them. Shame on you.
-I know.

00:54:58.363 --> 00:55:01.219
-She's calling you out.
-I will say… Oh, yeah, they do.

00:55:01.286 --> 00:55:04.504
Oh, I will say I've tried
to pick the best person.

00:55:04.571 --> 00:55:08.576
As far as keeping this nice,
friendly hometown store.

00:55:08.643 --> 00:55:10.033
It's not happening. Yeah.

00:55:10.100 --> 00:55:13.143
But the good thing is
we'll be right down the street.

00:55:13.210 --> 00:55:14.957
[Peter] Two blocks. Right there.

00:55:15.024 --> 00:55:17.691
I couldn't do two stores. I did want to.

00:55:18.283 --> 00:55:20.723
-But we won't be far.
-I'll come find ya.

00:55:20.790 --> 00:55:24.706
-That's wild. A lot of Americans
don't know the Patel story.

00:55:24.773 --> 00:55:25.606
Oh, it's bad.

00:55:25.673 --> 00:55:27.188
They got into hotels and then…

00:55:27.255 --> 00:55:30.063
Oh, they bought in
all the convenience stores.

00:55:30.130 --> 00:55:35.193
And the truth is,
is they are not personally invested here.

00:55:35.743 --> 00:55:39.313
So, again,
this is a very poverty-stricken area.

00:55:39.380 --> 00:55:41.930
People that aren't dealing with
their mental health.

00:55:41.997 --> 00:55:44.950
So you're going to see
more drug addicts, more alcoholics.

00:55:45.017 --> 00:55:47.290
So that's what they fill
their stores up with.

00:55:47.357 --> 00:55:48.945
Crack pipes, liquor.

00:55:49.550 --> 00:55:51.690
It's smart on a business side,

00:55:52.440 --> 00:55:56.366
if I was being honest
but very bad for the communities.

00:55:57.185 --> 00:55:59.423
The wild thing,
and I'm about to get into it…

00:55:59.490 --> 00:56:01.523
-Okay.
-…is we have this town, Lewiston.

00:56:01.590 --> 00:56:03.643
Sort of a small, compact place.

00:56:03.710 --> 00:56:09.123
And literally, almost a stone's throw away
is a very expensive college, right?

00:56:09.190 --> 00:56:10.913
Yes. Bates College.

00:56:10.980 --> 00:56:12.713
-Right there.
-Right there.

00:56:13.255 --> 00:56:16.116
-Do those students come into town ever?
-They actually do.

00:56:16.183 --> 00:56:19.718
They walk on Ash Street
down to Lisbon Street

00:56:19.785 --> 00:56:21.905
to go to, like, Forage Market, get coffee.

00:56:21.972 --> 00:56:24.876
And our restaurants
are about five blocks down.

00:56:24.943 --> 00:56:28.043
-But what a trip, having those worlds
right next to each other?

00:56:28.110 --> 00:56:30.427
Yeah. It's actually quite interesting.

00:56:30.494 --> 00:56:33.348
Bates College, I think,
is a great thing for Lewiston.

00:56:33.415 --> 00:56:36.296
It's wonderful to drive by
and see all the students.

00:56:36.363 --> 00:56:38.472
And it's a beautiful campus.

00:56:38.539 --> 00:56:40.187
But… Hey, Billy.

00:56:40.254 --> 00:56:42.644
I do believe that…

00:56:44.296 --> 00:56:47.996
I'm just going to put this out there.
Bates College should contribute more.

00:56:48.063 --> 00:56:51.037
-Well, they have an endowment
of almost a half billion.

00:56:51.104 --> 00:56:52.175
That's right.

00:56:52.242 --> 00:56:55.336
So it's like $90,000
for a kid to go to school there.

00:56:55.403 --> 00:56:58.763
Yeah. With tuition, boarding,
everything is 90-something, right?

00:56:58.830 --> 00:56:59.663
Yep.

00:56:59.730 --> 00:57:03.744
I mean, the admins and stuff
are making, like, over $500,000 a year.

00:57:03.811 --> 00:57:07.603
-So they don't contribute much?
I saw one of the mills said Bates on it.

00:57:07.670 --> 00:57:09.607
I don't think they do.

00:57:09.674 --> 00:57:12.132
-And you're on city council.
-I'm on city council.

00:57:12.199 --> 00:57:15.332
And that's one thing
that I will be fighting for

00:57:15.399 --> 00:57:19.342
is for Bates College
to contribute more to our tax base.

00:57:19.409 --> 00:57:22.102
They're buying up
a lot of properties around that area,

00:57:22.169 --> 00:57:23.587
a lot of residential homes,

00:57:23.654 --> 00:57:26.521
and multi-families
to house their students.

00:57:26.588 --> 00:57:28.563
And it comes off our tax base.

00:57:28.630 --> 00:57:30.230
-Do they get a tax break?

00:57:30.716 --> 00:57:32.327
-Yes.
-Because they're a college?

00:57:32.394 --> 00:57:33.449
They pay zero.

00:57:33.516 --> 00:57:34.779
-What?
-Nothing.

00:57:34.846 --> 00:57:35.750
-Come on.
-Nothing.

00:57:35.817 --> 00:57:38.002
-But it's not a public university.
-No.

00:57:38.942 --> 00:57:40.204
-But they get that tax break?
-Yes.

00:57:40.271 --> 00:57:42.530
-You sure on that?
-Positive.

00:57:44.352 --> 00:57:47.661
How they contribute,
which they feel is satisfactory,

00:57:47.728 --> 00:57:50.623
and I don't believe it's enough nearly,

00:57:51.266 --> 00:57:54.927
is their students
are to volunteer in our schools

00:57:54.994 --> 00:57:59.396
or some of the inner city organizations
that we have here.

00:58:00.778 --> 00:58:05.445
The students go and volunteer,
so they feel like that's payback enough.

00:58:06.690 --> 00:58:09.362
But they're, um,

00:58:10.038 --> 00:58:14.062
you know, the city is
plowing their streets,

00:58:14.129 --> 00:58:15.796
cleaning their sidewalks,

00:58:16.561 --> 00:58:20.923
fixing potholes on the streets
that they have basically bought

00:58:20.990 --> 00:58:24.643
all of the property on there
that is now off our tax base.

00:58:25.416 --> 00:58:28.791
So although I love Bates,
I think it's important

00:58:28.858 --> 00:58:30.651
they're doing zero, in my opinion,

00:58:30.718 --> 00:58:33.828
to financially contribute to our tax base.

00:58:34.540 --> 00:58:39.118
I've wanted to check out a few times,
you know, sell my property, sell my store,

00:58:39.185 --> 00:58:41.488
and spend time at home.

00:58:42.005 --> 00:58:43.783
And I've actually tried that.

00:58:43.850 --> 00:58:47.097
I'm most fulfilled
when I'm doing service work.

00:58:48.256 --> 00:58:51.282
I'm a huge humanitarian but

00:58:51.349 --> 00:58:54.549
I am huge on accountability
and having standards.

00:58:55.430 --> 00:58:57.753
-And you think we lost that?
-Oh, definitely.

00:58:57.820 --> 00:58:59.315
Definitely. We have.

00:58:59.382 --> 00:59:01.144
I think we need to bring that back.

00:59:01.211 --> 00:59:05.183
People need to start believing
in their communities again.

00:59:05.250 --> 00:59:08.403
I think we've lost hope, a lot of people.

00:59:08.980 --> 00:59:12.580
And until the older generation
gets their sh*t together

00:59:12.647 --> 00:59:17.455
and decides to go back
to what we know we need,

00:59:18.136 --> 00:59:19.748
nothing will change.

00:59:19.815 --> 00:59:22.193
-What do you mean by that,
the older generation?

00:59:22.260 --> 00:59:26.360
I think the older generation
really needs to, they've given up.

00:59:26.826 --> 00:59:28.948
They've given up
on the younger generation.

00:59:29.015 --> 00:59:30.455
-Like retirees or what?

00:59:30.522 --> 00:59:31.979
Yeah, I think.

00:59:32.046 --> 00:59:34.586
You know, they've lived their life.

00:59:34.653 --> 00:59:38.362
This is a much tougher generation
for lots of reasons.

00:59:38.429 --> 00:59:40.156
A lot more drug addiction, right?

00:59:40.223 --> 00:59:43.156
A lot more mental health
not being addressed.

00:59:44.010 --> 00:59:48.251
So they have given up.
They're kind of living their own lives.

00:59:48.318 --> 00:59:51.186
Let me just finish out my life in peace.

00:59:51.253 --> 00:59:54.053
But the truth is,
is if everybody gives up,

00:59:54.623 --> 00:59:58.046
then there's no one there
to teach this generation right from wrong.

00:59:58.113 --> 01:00:00.312
And that's what we've screwed up.

01:00:00.379 --> 01:00:01.246
Some of them.

01:00:02.825 --> 01:00:04.168
-This is your daughter.
-Yes.

01:00:04.235 --> 01:00:06.223
-She made the calendar.
-Yes, she did.

01:00:06.290 --> 01:00:07.717
-She's on the force here.

01:00:07.784 --> 01:00:08.995
-Right here in Lewiston.

01:00:09.062 --> 01:00:10.903
She's very well respected.

01:00:10.970 --> 01:00:12.503
-Do you worry about her?

01:00:13.230 --> 01:00:15.349
I do worry about her but

01:00:16.422 --> 01:00:19.516
I think that if I let that overcome me,

01:00:19.583 --> 01:00:22.084
I would discourage her
from being a police officer.

01:00:22.151 --> 01:00:24.359
And the truth is, is we need good ones.

01:00:24.426 --> 01:00:25.976
-She finds meaning in her work?

01:00:26.043 --> 01:00:27.529
-Oh, definitely.

01:00:27.596 --> 01:00:28.596
-That's awesome.

01:00:33.560 --> 01:00:34.699
Hey guys.

01:00:35.569 --> 01:00:37.534
-How are you?
-Are you guys missionaries?

01:00:37.601 --> 01:00:38.503
Yes, sir.

01:00:38.570 --> 01:00:40.473
-Any conversions in Lewiston?

01:00:40.540 --> 01:00:41.868
Yeah, quite a few.

01:00:41.935 --> 01:00:44.612
-Nice. Where are you guys from?
-I'm from Utah.

01:00:44.679 --> 01:00:47.246
-Okay. I've done a full video series
on Mormons.

01:00:47.313 --> 01:00:48.146
Oh, really?

01:00:48.213 --> 01:00:51.644
-I respect you guys because
you pump the pavement more than anyone.

01:00:51.711 --> 01:00:52.613
Appreciate it, man.

01:00:52.680 --> 01:00:54.197
You got any questions for us?

01:00:54.264 --> 01:00:56.388
-Yeah.
So how long are you in Lewiston for?

01:00:56.455 --> 01:00:59.496
So we are not absolutely sure
how long we're here for.

01:00:59.563 --> 01:01:02.518
We could stay here
for six weeks to six months,

01:01:02.585 --> 01:01:05.255
but we've been here for about a month.

01:01:05.322 --> 01:01:08.093
-Okay. Have you had
any problems on these streets?

01:01:08.160 --> 01:01:09.159
Not so far.

01:01:09.909 --> 01:01:11.153
-People are cool?

01:01:11.220 --> 01:01:12.672
I've heard they're dangerous.

01:01:12.739 --> 01:01:15.137
Generally, but just try to stay safe.

01:01:15.204 --> 01:01:17.761
-You know what?
You guys know how to take rejection.

01:01:17.828 --> 01:01:19.303
-You get a lot of no's.
-Yeah.

01:01:19.370 --> 01:01:21.342
Which is a great skill in life.

01:01:21.409 --> 01:01:24.211
-Yes, sir. It is.
-'Cause that's what life is.

01:01:25.237 --> 01:01:27.676
-Nice to meet you.
-Nice to meet you, too.

01:01:27.743 --> 01:01:29.943
-Have a good rest of your day.
-You, too.

01:01:30.010 --> 01:01:31.343
-Take care.
-Have fun.

01:01:36.666 --> 01:01:38.066
What up, brother man?

01:01:39.246 --> 01:01:40.693
-How you doing?
-Good.

01:01:40.760 --> 01:01:43.461
You're just fired up.
I thought you were somebody else.

01:01:43.528 --> 01:01:45.793
-So tell me your name again.
-Peter Santenello.

01:01:45.860 --> 01:01:47.825
-Peter Santenello.
-I'm Brian Witham.

01:01:47.892 --> 01:01:50.482
-Brian Witham.
-From Portland, Munjoy Hill.

01:01:50.549 --> 01:01:51.777
Okay. That's nice.

01:01:51.844 --> 01:01:54.145
And I have been in this trap before.

01:01:54.770 --> 01:01:57.842
I, right now, currently,
am doing very well.

01:01:57.909 --> 01:01:58.742
-Good for you.

01:01:58.809 --> 01:02:03.483
And, uh, but I know everything
and anything about Lewiston's

01:02:05.282 --> 01:02:07.201
corruptive, uh,

01:02:08.007 --> 01:02:11.599
addictive ways in this community.

01:02:11.666 --> 01:02:13.593
-What do you mean by corruptive?

01:02:13.660 --> 01:02:15.003
Drugs, alcohol.

01:02:15.070 --> 01:02:18.770
Not corrupt as in politics or anything.

01:02:18.837 --> 01:02:20.197
But, yeah.

01:02:20.636 --> 01:02:23.436
I know how bad this town
can take you down.

01:02:24.649 --> 01:02:26.467
Oh, my God, baby.

01:02:26.534 --> 01:02:29.185
I just got back last night from Florida.

01:02:29.252 --> 01:02:32.785
-Sorry. I wasn't going to bother you.
-No, I'm talking to Pete Santenello.

01:02:32.852 --> 01:02:34.807
-Yeah.
-I'll be right in.

01:02:35.427 --> 01:02:37.321
-You look beautiful.
-Thank you.

01:02:37.388 --> 01:02:39.843
-You're a charmer.
You're a natural charmer.

01:02:39.910 --> 01:02:41.955
I am a natural good guy.

01:02:42.022 --> 01:02:43.254
-Cool.
-That's what I am.

01:02:43.321 --> 01:02:44.911
-You're off the drugs now?
-I am.

01:02:44.978 --> 01:02:47.770
-One hundred percent.
-Good for you. Keep, keep strong.

01:02:47.837 --> 01:02:50.629
Been back and forth a bazillion times.

01:02:51.300 --> 01:02:52.866
-Um…
-Stay strong.

01:02:52.933 --> 01:02:56.549
I've been as skinny
and wrecked out as anybody here.

01:02:56.616 --> 01:02:59.464
So, I never, ever look down on anybody

01:03:00.019 --> 01:03:01.619
because I've been there.

01:03:02.350 --> 01:03:04.380
I don't even know
where I'm going with any of this.

01:03:04.447 --> 01:03:06.046
It's all right.
Probably sounds stupid, but…

01:03:06.113 --> 01:03:06.947
-No, it's good.

01:03:07.014 --> 01:03:10.687
God bless and, uh, you can get better.

01:03:10.754 --> 01:03:12.533
You can. I've done it.

01:03:12.600 --> 01:03:13.913
Proven it. Pete.

01:03:13.980 --> 01:03:15.724
-Yes. You be good.
-You be good.

01:03:15.791 --> 01:03:18.080
-All right.
-Keep at it. Congrats.

01:03:23.132 --> 01:03:27.638
All right, guys. We're literally
two minutes from Susan's store

01:03:27.705 --> 01:03:29.474
and this is Bates College.

01:03:29.541 --> 01:03:34.766
Quite an expensive experience,
though I believe roughly half the students

01:03:34.833 --> 01:03:36.743
have some sort of financial aid.

01:03:36.810 --> 01:03:39.048
But how crazy is this?

01:03:39.115 --> 01:03:40.700
Right up the road.

01:03:41.309 --> 01:03:47.587
And like I said earlier, an endowment
of almost a half billion dollars.

01:03:49.469 --> 01:03:51.835
What's going on with the tents here?

01:03:51.902 --> 01:03:53.373
Definitely not homeless.

01:03:53.440 --> 01:03:57.641
And it's roughly 1800 students
that go to school here.

01:03:57.708 --> 01:03:58.593
That's it.

01:03:58.660 --> 01:04:00.260
Summer break, obviously.

01:04:00.762 --> 01:04:02.253
So not many around.

01:04:02.320 --> 01:04:03.621
-Nice dog, man.
-Thanks.

01:04:03.688 --> 01:04:05.621
-In training.
-Just to be a good boy.

01:04:05.688 --> 01:04:06.600
Oh, okay.

01:04:06.667 --> 01:04:08.434
Sit. Good job.

01:04:08.501 --> 01:04:10.139
-Beautiful dog.
-Thank you.

01:04:10.206 --> 01:04:11.385
And his name's Hulk.

01:04:11.452 --> 01:04:12.479
-Hulk?
-Yeah.

01:04:12.546 --> 01:04:14.226
What's going on with the tents?

01:04:14.293 --> 01:04:16.543
I got no clue, homie.
I just showed up myself.

01:04:16.610 --> 01:04:19.077
-Do you live here?
-I live up the road.

01:04:19.145 --> 01:04:22.901
It's a lot cleaner than the
rest of the area, so not as much trash.

01:04:23.035 --> 01:04:25.218
-It's like
a totally different world up here.

01:04:25.285 --> 01:04:26.515
Yeah. Yeah.

01:04:26.582 --> 01:04:28.315
It's definitely different.

01:04:29.715 --> 01:04:31.415
-How you doing?
-Good, how are you?

01:04:31.482 --> 01:04:33.096
-Good. What's up with the tents?

01:04:33.163 --> 01:04:36.323
Oh, the trek across Maine
and some people camp.

01:04:36.391 --> 01:04:37.964
So we're just doing a big loop.

01:04:38.031 --> 01:04:38.864
Oh, cool.

01:04:38.931 --> 01:04:41.281
Some people stay in the dorms,
some people camp.

01:04:41.348 --> 01:04:43.363
-And the college lets you stay here?
-Yeah.

01:04:43.430 --> 01:04:44.653
-That's awesome.

01:04:44.720 --> 01:04:47.889
Yeah. We stay at St. Joseph's College
tomorrow night.

01:04:47.956 --> 01:04:49.346
-How's it going so far?
-Good.

01:04:49.413 --> 01:04:52.053
Sixty… how many? Sixty-eight miles today

01:04:52.120 --> 01:04:54.918
to support the American Lung Association.

01:04:54.985 --> 01:04:56.118
Stop teen vaping.

01:04:57.015 --> 01:04:59.111
-Right. That's a problem, huh?
-Yeah.

01:04:59.178 --> 01:05:00.626
I have teenagers, so…

01:05:00.693 --> 01:05:02.828
How do you keep them from vaping?

01:05:02.895 --> 01:05:03.737
We don't.

01:05:03.804 --> 01:05:08.032
We try and pass legislation
to keep it, like, away.

01:05:08.099 --> 01:05:10.581
-Is it a big problem right now?
The teen vaping stuff?

01:05:10.648 --> 01:05:11.871
I think so. Yeah.

01:05:11.938 --> 01:05:15.353
I'm a physician and I see
a lot of teenagers vape. It's really sad.

01:05:15.420 --> 01:05:16.835
-You know what's interesting?

01:05:16.902 --> 01:05:20.065
I was just talking to this woman, Susan,
who owns a supermarket.

01:05:20.132 --> 01:05:21.334
-She's selling it.
-Yeah.

01:05:21.401 --> 01:05:25.772
One of the problems in places
like Lewiston is the new owner comes in,

01:05:26.199 --> 01:05:28.568
takes out the fruits and vegetables
and the meat,

01:05:28.635 --> 01:05:32.058
puts in the vapes and pipes and weed

01:05:32.125 --> 01:05:34.807
and all that stuff,
and you're like, it's not working.

01:05:34.874 --> 01:05:37.693
I understand that small businesses
have to make a living.

01:05:37.760 --> 01:05:40.627
But sadly,
it's at the expense of our youth.

01:05:41.325 --> 01:05:43.800
-Yeah.
-But I don't know how you fix that.

01:05:43.867 --> 01:05:45.531
-Well, you're trying.
-I'm trying.

01:05:45.598 --> 01:05:49.210
I'm raising money for the American
Lung Association, Biking Across Maine.

01:05:49.277 --> 01:05:50.614
Cool. I love cycling, too.

01:05:51.470 --> 01:05:55.436
All right, guys, for 1,800 students,
this place goes on for a ways.

01:05:55.503 --> 01:05:58.963
Beautiful buildings,
just giving you that nice

01:05:59.030 --> 01:06:02.093
Northeast college experience.

01:06:02.160 --> 01:06:05.101
It's like everything lifted
by getting out of

01:06:06.377 --> 01:06:07.771
Downtown Lewiston.

01:06:08.365 --> 01:06:10.904
Though I enjoyed my time there.
Met some good people.

01:06:10.971 --> 01:06:13.353
It's heavy. I mean, I can't sugarcoat it.

01:06:13.420 --> 01:06:15.308
It's… heavy.

01:06:15.375 --> 01:06:18.056
There's a lot of drug use
and you can sort of feel it.

01:06:18.123 --> 01:06:22.487
And then you're in this environment
and there's none of that going on.

01:06:23.480 --> 01:06:24.942
And they're so close,

01:06:25.608 --> 01:06:27.128
yet so far.

01:06:27.195 --> 01:06:29.523
And it's easy.
We all do it. We stay in our bubbles.

01:06:29.590 --> 01:06:34.733
No matter if you're in the nicest
neighborhood or the worst neighborhood,

01:06:34.800 --> 01:06:37.648
it's just you're in your bubble,
in your environment.

01:06:37.715 --> 01:06:41.135
And it's tough to see
the other environment often.

01:06:41.995 --> 01:06:44.498
It could be super close by, like here.

01:06:44.565 --> 01:06:48.976
So I don't think too many people
are coming from Lewiston up to here.

01:06:49.043 --> 01:06:51.116
But students here,

01:06:51.648 --> 01:06:54.928
Susan said they were
helping with some things in town,

01:06:54.995 --> 01:06:56.914
but she brought up a good point.

01:06:56.981 --> 01:07:00.713
Like, what can a place like this
do that's super wealthy

01:07:01.620 --> 01:07:04.953
with such a high tuition
and such a huge endowment?

01:07:05.642 --> 01:07:09.993
How can they help a place like Lewiston
if it's just right there?

01:07:10.715 --> 01:07:13.315
Yeah, it just doesn't feel good today.

01:07:13.382 --> 01:07:15.671
The combination of those two

01:07:16.877 --> 01:07:18.677
right next to one another.

01:07:19.759 --> 01:07:21.412
I'm pretty hardened.

01:07:21.479 --> 01:07:24.383
I've done a lot of traveling
and seen a lot of the world.

01:07:24.450 --> 01:07:27.344
Every once in a while
it gets me a bit. This…

01:07:27.411 --> 01:07:28.244
Um…

01:07:29.450 --> 01:07:30.814
Yeah, it doesn't sit right.

01:07:30.881 --> 01:07:32.846
It doesn't feel good.

01:07:32.913 --> 01:07:37.251
And we've got so much
amazingness in this country.

01:07:37.318 --> 01:07:39.718
So much beauty. So much opportunity.

01:07:40.994 --> 01:07:44.231
And then you see Downtown Lewiston
and you're like,

01:07:44.298 --> 01:07:47.578
God, we've got some places
really limping along.

01:07:47.645 --> 01:07:50.713
A lot of people on drugs.
A lot of people suffering.

01:07:50.779 --> 01:07:54.357
And I would love to see,
and many of you would love to see,

01:07:54.424 --> 01:07:57.014
more of a focus on
this from our government.

01:07:57.081 --> 01:07:59.103
It doesn't matter the party.

01:07:59.170 --> 01:08:01.051
Money's not a simple fix.

01:08:01.118 --> 01:08:01.951
Um…

01:08:03.800 --> 01:08:06.954
It's going to take a lot,
but it's important.

01:08:07.048 --> 01:08:09.512
And to me,
the reason I want to show these stories

01:08:09.579 --> 01:08:12.348
is just to bring attention to them
because I do think it's

01:08:12.972 --> 01:08:17.498
one of the most important things
going on in this country right now

01:08:17.565 --> 01:08:21.510
is the fact that we've got
a lot of people falling out.

01:08:21.577 --> 01:08:23.133
And I don't want to be negative.

01:08:23.200 --> 01:08:24.314
I really don't.

01:08:24.381 --> 01:08:26.714
But I'm just telling you how it is.

01:08:32.370 --> 01:08:34.443
[woman] We order flights. We share them.

01:08:38.150 --> 01:08:39.262
What do you got going?

01:08:39.329 --> 01:08:41.693
A chicken enchilada
with a red guajillo sauce.

01:08:41.760 --> 01:08:43.382
-Rice and beans.
-Nice.

01:08:43.449 --> 01:08:44.936
Are you living the American dream?

01:08:45.003 --> 01:08:46.810
-I am living it.
-That's cool.

01:08:51.312 --> 01:08:52.837
All right. Thank you, brother.

01:08:52.904 --> 01:08:56.431
And then the green sauce is the mild.
The red one's a hotter one.

01:08:56.985 --> 01:08:58.390
-Red one's hot?
-Yeah.

01:08:58.457 --> 01:09:01.737
Just like, try a little bit of a chip
before you go crazy, okay?

01:09:01.804 --> 01:09:02.637
Thank you.

01:09:06.123 --> 01:09:08.485
All right. Chicken quesadilla.

01:09:14.210 --> 01:09:15.043
Mmm.

01:09:17.776 --> 01:09:18.609
Very cheesy.

01:09:22.850 --> 01:09:24.183
Good tender chicken.

01:09:30.384 --> 01:09:31.451
That's got kick.

01:09:32.997 --> 01:09:34.313
What an interesting place.

01:09:34.380 --> 01:09:36.984
Now this was

01:09:38.183 --> 01:09:40.225
not even a full day in Lewiston.

01:09:40.292 --> 01:09:43.283
So impossible to give you the full story.

01:09:44.176 --> 01:09:45.488
It really is.

01:09:45.555 --> 01:09:47.672
There's a lot to this place.
I can feel it.

01:09:47.739 --> 01:09:50.386
There's a lot said off camera today

01:09:50.453 --> 01:09:54.334
that I can't totally confirm,
unfortunately.

01:09:54.401 --> 01:09:56.353
Like, I just don't know for sure.

01:09:56.873 --> 01:10:01.492
But there's a big story here,
and in due time I'm sure it will surface.

01:10:01.559 --> 01:10:04.635
But there's some
hoarder in charge, let's say,

01:10:04.702 --> 01:10:08.125
that sort of has
people under their thumbs.

01:10:09.090 --> 01:10:10.458
And then there's the…

01:10:11.810 --> 01:10:15.937
out at the nice restaurant,
Mexican restaurant.

01:10:16.004 --> 01:10:17.926
People having a good time.

01:10:17.993 --> 01:10:20.917
We got a beautiful college up the street.

01:10:20.984 --> 01:10:24.003
I'm sure there's all sorts of
other great people here

01:10:24.070 --> 01:10:25.714
and cool things happening.

01:10:25.781 --> 01:10:28.495
So, like all of these stories,

01:10:29.414 --> 01:10:34.024
they're complicated,
and way more mixed up than we ever think.

01:10:34.870 --> 01:10:36.599
So, I've enjoyed my day, actually.

01:10:36.666 --> 01:10:38.067
I've met great people.

01:10:38.995 --> 01:10:41.630
Susan was fantastic too at the end there.

01:10:42.108 --> 01:10:44.891
And you gotta love people like her
really giving it.

01:10:44.958 --> 01:10:48.302
You know, she can tap out,
not have to deal with

01:10:48.849 --> 01:10:51.689
what she has to deal with,
but she wants to help.

01:10:51.756 --> 01:10:55.289
And people like that
keep a community like this going.

01:10:55.793 --> 01:10:58.148
Also, this couple here
is doing their thing.

01:10:58.215 --> 01:11:00.274
So, it's been a cool day.

01:11:09.065 --> 01:11:11.628
All right, guys.
That about does it for today.

01:11:11.695 --> 01:11:14.450
Hope to see you in early August
on my book tour.

01:11:14.517 --> 01:11:17.543
If you're in one of those cities
or near one of those cities.

01:11:17.610 --> 01:11:19.198
More dates coming too.

01:11:19.265 --> 01:11:21.385
QR Link down below.

01:11:22.169 --> 01:11:25.743
And also, this is part of
a Greater Maine series.

01:11:25.810 --> 01:11:27.888
Five stories in total.

01:11:27.955 --> 01:11:29.478
Five different Maines.

01:11:30.077 --> 01:11:32.607
Gonna have one of those videos
around here.

01:11:32.674 --> 01:11:34.651
Thanks for coming on that journey.

01:11:34.719 --> 01:11:35.986
Until the next one.

01:11:36.052 --> 01:11:38.023
[blues music]

